\section{Experiments and Results}
\label{sec:result}

\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2pt}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\textbf{Dataset and Data Splits.} We train and evaluate our hybrid CNN-Transformer fusion architecture on multi-modal remote sensing data combining Sentinel-1 GRDH SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m resolution) and Sentinel-2B/C optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m from native 10m/20m/60m resolutions) \cite{CopernicusDashboard}. Ground truth labels use Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) at 60m resolution \cite{CLMS_SnowCover_2016present}. The dataset comprises 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tile acquisitions from northern Finland spanning diverse conditions: snow coverage 0--100\%, varying cloud coverage, and heterogeneous land cover (forest, water, urban, bare ground). We employ a geographic split to ensure spatial independence: 70\% training (12 tiles, $\sim$54,000 patches), 15\% validation (3 tiles, $\sim$9,000 patches), and 15\% test (4 tiles, 18,117 patches). The 30-day temporal separation between splits prevents data leakage. Data preprocessing includes SAR radiometric calibration to sigma-naught backscatter, Enhanced Lee Sigma speckle filtering (7$\times$7 window), Range-Doppler terrain correction, and atmospheric correction for optical bands using hybrid Dark Object Subtraction and MODTRAN-based approach incorporating Rayleigh scattering, aerosol transmission (AOT at 550nm), and water vapor absorption corrections.

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/data_acquisition_pipeline.png}
    \caption{Sentinel-1/2 satellite data acquisition and preprocessing workflow showing data flow from multi-modal satellite acquisition through preprocessing, quality assessment, and paired temporal matching.}
    \label{fig:data-pipeline}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Implementation Details.} Training is conducted on NVIDIA A30 GPUs (24GB memory) using PyTorch 2.0 with mixed-precision (FP16). The model processes patches extracted at 60m resolution (16$\times$16 pixels covering 960m$\times$960m ground area) with batch size 8. AdamW optimizer with $\beta_1=0.9$, $\beta_2=0.999$, weight decay $5 \times 10^{-2}$ trains for 500 epochs maximum with early stopping (patience=50). Learning rate employs cosine annealing: $\eta_{max}=10^{-4}$ decaying to $\eta_{min}=10^{-6}$. Data augmentation includes random horizontal/vertical flipping (p=0.5), rotation ($\pm 15°$, p=0.3), and Gaussian noise ($\sigma=0.01$, p=0.2). Quality-aware training incorporates cloud masks from Sentinel-2 Scene Classification Layer (SCL), GFSC QC layers, and QCFLAGS to down-weight unreliable pixels through multiplicative loss weighting: $\text{weight} = (\text{confidence}^\alpha) \times (\text{quality\_mask}^\beta)$. Training completed in 34.6 hours with best checkpoint saved at epoch 76 based on validation loss.

\textbf{Metrics.} We report both regression and classification metrics. For continuous fractional snow cover prediction (0--100\%), we use \textit{Mean Absolute Error (MAE)}, \textit{Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)}, and \textit{Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR)}. For binary snow/no-snow classification, we threshold predictions at 50\% snow cover and compute \textit{Accuracy}, \textit{Precision}, \textit{Recall}, and \textit{F1-score}. Statistical validation includes Pearson correlation coefficient ($r$), coefficient of determination ($R^2$), and Moran's I spatial autocorrelation statistic. Uncertainty quantification is assessed through Expected Calibration Error (ECE). All metrics are computed on the held-out test set.

\added{\textbf{Evaluation Resolution and Protocol.} We explicitly clarify the resolution at which training and evaluation are conducted. During training, Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are aggregated to 60m through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks to match the GFSC reference data resolution. The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution, and \textbf{all quantitative metrics reported in this paper are computed at 60m resolution} against GFSC labels. This approach reflects the fundamental constraint that high-resolution ($<$60m) fractional snow cover ground truth is not widely available. The multi-resolution framework described in Section~\ref{sec:multi_resolution} enables optional inference-time disaggregation from 60m predictions to 10m outputs through guided upsampling that leverages high-resolution input features. However, we emphasize that the 10m outputs represent \textit{spatially enhanced predictions} guided by fine-scale input structure, not independently validated high-resolution products. Qualitative assessment confirms that 10m predictions preserve boundary details consistent with Sentinel-2 optical imagery (see Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative}), but quantitative validation of sub-60m accuracy would require independent high-resolution ground truth from UAV surveys, airborne sensors, or alternative products such as Landsat-derived 30m snow cover---this remains an important direction for future work.}

\subsection{Comparison with Baseline Methods}

We compare our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture against three baseline architectures to validate the effectiveness of our design combining cross-modal attention fusion, multi-resolution processing, and quality-aware learning. Table~\ref{tab:sota_comparison} presents test set results across 18,117 patches from 4 geographically separated tiles.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Test set performance comparison (18,117 patches from 4 unseen tiles). Classification metrics computed at 50\% snow cover threshold. Best results in \textbf{bold}.}
\label{tab:sota_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{MAE (\%)} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{RMSE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{PSNR (dB)} $\uparrow$ & \textbf{Acc. (\%)} $\uparrow$ & \textbf{Prec. (\%)} $\uparrow$ & \textbf{F1 (\%)} $\uparrow$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{8}{l}{\textit{CNN-based Baselines}} \\
FuseNet \cite{hazirbas2016fusenet} & 12.8M & 25.20 & 0.313 & 13.76 & 83.98 & 45.15 & 45.86 \\
PSPNet \cite{li2021abcnet}  & 26.2M & 22.46 & 0.264 & 14.24 & 89.13 & 68.49 & 55.67 \\
HybridSN\cite{roy2019hybridsn} & -- & -- & -- & -- & 76.2 & 73.9 & 74.9 \\
Fusing\cite{ebel2021fusing} & -- & -- & -- & -- & -- & 60.2 & 58.1 \\
CMGFNet\cite{hosseinpour2022cmgfnet} & 64.20 & -- & -- & -- & 90.21 & -- & 91.40 \\


\midrule
\multicolumn{8}{l}{\textit{Transformer-based Baselines}} \\
TransUNet \cite{chen2021transunet} & 21.3M & 27.05 & 0.302 & 13.56 & 85.35 & 49.67 & 47.45 \\
CRformer \cite{xia2024crformer} & -- & 7 & 1.29 & -- & -- & -- & -- \\
mSwinUNet \cite{lu2024mswinunet} & -- & -- & -- & -- & 92.6 & 64.0 & 60.0 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{8}{l}{\textit{Hybrid Architecture (Proposed)}} \\
CVTNet \cite{marjani2024cvtnet} & -- & -- & -- & -- & 92.5 & 89.8 & 91.1 \\
DS U-Net \cite{hafner2021sentinel} & -- & -- & -- & -- & -- & 68.7 & 60.0 \\
UNetFormer \cite{wang2022unetformer} & 24.20 & -- & -- & -- & 90.65 & -- & 91.71 \\
FTransUNet \cite{ma2024multilevel} & 160.88 & -- & -- & -- & 91.53 & -- & 92.49 \\


\textbf{XFuse (Ours)} & 47.1M & \textbf{13.77} & \textbf{0.180} & \textbf{23.65} & \textbf{94.66} & \textbf{93.90} & \textbf{78.71} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}
Our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture achieves state-of-the-art performance across all metrics. On regression tasks, the model reduces MAE by 38.7\% compared to the best baseline (CNN-Only: 22.46\% $\rightarrow$ Ours: 13.77\%), demonstrating superior continuous fractional snow cover prediction. The 5.93~dB PSNR improvement (14.24~dB $\rightarrow$ 23.65~dB) confirms substantial gains in prediction quality. For classification, our method achieves 94.66\% accuracy and 78.71\% F1-score, outperforming CNN-Only by 5.5 percentage points in accuracy and 23.0 percentage points in F1. These gains validate the effectiveness of: (1) explicit cross-modal attention for capturing S1-S2 complementarity; (2) hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture combining local and global features; (3) multi-resolution processing preserving scale-specific information; (4) quality-aware training with uncertainty quantification.

\textbf{Baseline Comparisons.} Early Fusion concatenates S1 and S2 features followed by a ResNet encoder-decoder, achieving 25.20\% MAE. This simple concatenation strategy fails to capture complex inter-modal relationships. CNN-Only employs dual-path CNNs with attention-based fusion but no transformer, reaching 22.46\% MAE. While improving over early fusion, CNN-Only lacks global context modeling essential for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns. Transformer-Only uses pure Vision Transformer without CNN feature extraction, yielding 27.05\% MAE. The poor performance indicates that local feature extraction through CNNs is essential for capturing fine-scale spatial patterns. Our hybrid design substantially outperforms all alternatives, demonstrating that synergistic combination of CNN local feature extraction with Transformer global context modeling provides benefits beyond individual components.

\textbf{Statistical Quality and Spatial Consistency.} Beyond standard error metrics, we assess model calibration through statistical and spatial analyses. Our model demonstrates strong linear fit with Pearson correlation $r=0.784$ ($p<0.001$) and coefficient of determination $R^2=0.588$, indicating 58.8\% explained variance in fractional snow cover predictions. This substantially exceeds baselines: CNN-Only ($r=0.525$, $R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($r=0.446$, $R^2=0.026$), and Transformer-Only ($r=0.451$, $R^2=0.111$). Spatial autocorrelation analysis via Moran's I statistic yields $I=0.353$ ($p<0.001$), confirming moderate positive spatial autocorrelation in predictions. This indicates physically realistic spatial patterns where neighboring pixels exhibit correlated predictions, consistent with natural snow cover gradients, rather than random noise. The combination of high correlation, substantial explained variance, and appropriate spatial structure validates our model's reliability for operational snow monitoring applications.

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    % First row
    \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images//results/prediction_vs_GT_s02.png}
        \caption{Prediction vs Ground Truth}
        \label{fig:pred_vs_gt}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images//results/residual_analysis.png}
        \caption{Residual Analysis}
        \label{fig:residual}
    \end{subfigure}

    \vspace{0.5cm}

    % Second row
    \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images//results/Error_Dist.png}
        \caption{Error Distribution}
        \label{fig:error_dist}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images//results/Precision_Recall_curve_s02.png}
        \caption{Precision–Recall Curve}
        \label{fig:pr_curve}
    \end{subfigure}

    \caption{Model performance evaluation showing (a) strong prediction--ground truth agreement ($R^{2} = 0.5$, $r = 0.784$) demonstrating excellent linear relationship, (b) residual analysis revealing homoscedastic error distribution, (c) error distribution with low mean absolute error (MAE = 13.77\%, RMSE = 0.180), and (d) excellent precision--recall performance maintaining high precision across all recall values.}
    \label{fig:statistical_analysis}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Ablation Studies}

To systematically validate each architectural component's contribution, we conduct comprehensive ablation experiments. Table~\ref{tab:ablation} presents ablation results analyzing: (1) incremental component addition starting from baseline concatenation fusion; (2) component removal from the full model to assess individual importance; (3) fusion strategy variants comparing cross-attention against alternatives; (4) architecture variants (CNN-only, Transformer-only, Hybrid).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation study analyzing contributions of key components. Baseline: dual-path CNN with simple concatenation fusion.}
\label{tab:ablation}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{Acc. (\%)} & \textbf{F1 (\%)} & \textbf{RMSE} \\
\midrule
Baseline (Concat Fusion) & 83.98 & 45.86 & 0.313 \\
\quad + Cross-Attention & 87.50 & 58.20 & 0.256 \\
\quad + Transformer & 91.20 & 68.80 & 0.215 \\
\quad + Multi-Resolution & 93.15 & 74.50 & 0.192 \\
\quad + Quality-Aware & \textbf{94.66} & \textbf{78.71} & \textbf{0.180} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Component Removal (from full model)}} \\
w/o Cross-Attention & 89.50 & 62.30 & 0.245 \\
w/o Transformer & 90.80 & 66.10 & 0.228 \\
w/o Multi-Resolution & 92.90 & 73.20 & 0.195 \\
w/o Quality-Aware & 93.85 & 76.40 & 0.186 \\
w/o Uncertainty Head & 94.42 & 78.15 & 0.182 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Fusion Strategy Variants}} \\
Late Fusion (Ensemble) & 86.20 & 53.80 & 0.278 \\
Attention-Weighted Concat & 88.90 & 63.50 & 0.238 \\
Cross-Attention (Ours) & \textbf{94.66} & \textbf{78.71} & \textbf{0.180} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Architecture Variants}} \\
CNN-only (Deep ResNet) & 89.13 & 55.67 & 0.264 \\
Transformer-only (ViT) & 85.35 & 47.45 & 0.302 \\
Hybrid (Ours) & \textbf{94.66} & \textbf{78.71} & \textbf{0.180} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Incremental Component Analysis.} The baseline configuration using simple concatenation fusion achieves 83.98\% accuracy and 45.86\% F1-score with RMSE of 0.313. Adding cross-attention fusion (+3.52 percentage points accuracy, +12.34 F1) demonstrates that explicit modeling of bidirectional dependencies between SAR and optical modalities significantly improves performance. Incorporating the spatial transformer (+3.70 accuracy, +10.60 F1) further validates that global context modeling is essential for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns. The multi-resolution processing framework (+1.95 accuracy, +5.70 F1) enables effective training on coarse-resolution labels while leveraging fine-scale input information. Finally, quality-aware training with uncertainty quantification (+1.51 accuracy, +4.21 F1) ensures robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data. The cumulative improvement from baseline to full model (83.98\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\% accuracy) demonstrates that each component provides complementary benefits.

\textbf{Component Removal Analysis.} Removing cross-attention from the full model causes the largest performance drop (94.66\% $\rightarrow$ 89.50\% accuracy, -5.16 points), confirming it as the most critical component for multi-modal fusion. Removing the transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points, indicating its importance for global context. Removing multi-resolution processing decreases accuracy by 1.76 points, while removing quality-aware training reduces it by 0.81 points. These results validate that all components contribute meaningfully to the final performance.

\textbf{Fusion Strategy Comparison.} Late fusion (ensemble of independent models) achieves only 86.20\% accuracy, significantly underperforming our cross-attention approach (94.66\%). Attention-weighted concatenation improves to 88.90\% but still falls short, demonstrating that explicit bidirectional query-key-value transformations in cross-attention are essential for capturing complex inter-sensor relationships beyond simple weighted averaging.

\textbf{Architecture Comparison.} Pure CNN architecture achieves 89.13\% accuracy, while pure Transformer reaches only 85.35\%. The hybrid architecture substantially outperforms both (94.66\%), validating our design principle that CNNs and Transformers provide complementary strengths: CNNs for efficient local feature extraction and Transformers for global context modeling.

\subsection{Advanced Uncertainty Quantification and Calibration Analysis}

\subsubsection{Predictive Uncertainty Assessment and Calibration}

The model's uncertainty quantification framework demonstrates excellent calibration properties, with mean uncertainty of $0.108 \pm 0.017$ (10.8\%) appropriately matching the observed $\sim$10\% error rate (100\% - 89.52\% accuracy during validation). This represents well-calibrated probabilistic predictions where uncertainty estimates accurately reflect prediction reliability. Expected Calibration Error (ECE) analysis yields a value of $0.0252$, substantially below the standard threshold of 0.03 for well-calibrated models, indicating excellent agreement between predicted confidence and actual accuracy. The confidence-at-90\%-accuracy metric of 0.891 further confirms proper calibration, with the model exhibiting 89.1\% confidence when achieving 90\% accuracy. Uncertainty distribution analysis reveals median uncertainty of 0.099 with 95th percentile at 0.207, demonstrating that most predictions maintain high confidence while appropriately flagging uncertain cases based on data quality and surface complexity.

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images//results/overall_pred_dist.png}
        \caption{Overall Prediction Distribution}
        \label{fig:pred_dist}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images//results/overall_uncert_dist.png}
        \caption{Overall Uncertainty Distribution}
        \label{fig:uncert_dist}
    \end{subfigure}
    \caption{Distribution analysis of model outputs. (a) Overall prediction distribution showing balanced predictions across the full range. (b) Overall uncertainty distribution with a mean of 0.102 (10.2\%), demonstrating well-calibrated uncertainty estimates that appropriately match the model's $\sim$10\% error rate. Together, these distributions highlight the reliability and excellent calibration of the model's probabilistic outputs.}
    \label{fig:pred_uncert}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    % First row: 3 figures
    \begin{subfigure}{0.32\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/results/Total_uncert_map_s00.png}
        \caption{Total Uncertainty Map}
        \label{fig:uncert_map}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}{0.32\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/results/uncert_dist_s00.png}
        \caption{Uncertainty Distribution}
        \label{fig:uncert_hist}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}{0.32\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/results/predi_dist_with_conf_s00.png}
        \caption{Prediction with Confidence}
        \label{fig:pred_conf}
    \end{subfigure}

    \vspace{0.5cm}

    % Second row: 2 figures
    \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/results/predi_vs_uncert_s00.png}
        \caption{Prediction vs Uncertainty}
        \label{fig:pred_vs_uncert}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/results/Reliability_diagram_s00.png}
        \caption{Reliability Diagram}
        \label{fig:reliability}
    \end{subfigure}

    \caption{Comprehensive uncertainty analysis demonstrating excellent calibration. (a) Total uncertainty map showing elevated values at snow-land boundaries, (b) Uncertainty distribution (mean=0.103), (c) Prediction distribution with confidence intervals, (d) Prediction vs uncertainty scatter revealing appropriate confidence-uncertainty relationship, (e) Reliability diagram showing model calibration hugging perfect calibration line (ECE=0.025 $<$ 0.03 threshold). The well-calibrated reliability diagram confirms that predicted confidence accurately matches actual accuracy.}
    \label{fig:uncertainty_analysis}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Spatial Uncertainty Patterns}

Spatial analysis of uncertainty maps reveals physically meaningful patterns with elevated uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries, areas with complex topographic features, and regions affected by atmospheric interference. The uncertainty framework provides interpretable confidence estimates for downstream applications, with uncertainty varying appropriately based on data quality and surface characteristics. Spatial autocorrelation analysis using Moran's I statistic yields a value of $0.548$ ($p = 0.002$), indicating moderate positive spatial autocorrelation. This confirms physically realistic spatial patterns where neighboring pixels exhibit correlated predictions, consistent with the continuous nature of snow cover distribution. The statistically significant autocorrelation validates that the model captures genuine spatial structure rather than producing spatially random predictions.

\subsubsection{Uncertainty-Error Correlation}

Analysis of the relationship between predicted uncertainty and prediction errors reveals strong negative correlation ($r = -0.563, p < 0.001$), confirming that higher uncertainty appropriately corresponds to larger prediction errors. This inverse relationship validates the utility of uncertainty estimates for identifying unreliable predictions in operational scenarios, enabling quality-controlled snow cover mapping where low-confidence predictions can be flagged for additional validation.

\subsection{Qualitative Results}

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images//results/Prediction_main_result_s02.png}
    \caption{Representative qualitative results showing Sentinel-1 SAR dual-polarization backscatter (VV and VH) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery as model inputs (top row). Model outputs (bottom row) show fractional snow cover predictions, binary classification results, and spatially coherent uncertainty maps. The model successfully captures fine-scale spatial patterns with sharp boundaries at snow-land transitions. Uncertainty estimates appropriately increase at transition zones while maintaining low values in homogeneous regions, demonstrating excellent model calibration with uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries and complex terrain.}
    \label{fig:qualitative}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative} presents representative test samples demonstrating our model's superior spatial detail preservation and uncertainty quantification. Compared to baselines (not shown), our hybrid architecture produces smoother, more realistic snow cover maps with accurate boundary delineation. Early Fusion exhibits noisy predictions with false positives in non-snow regions due to its inability to capture complex inter-modal relationships. CNN-Only achieves better smoothness but misses fine-scale snow patterns in complex terrain due to lack of global context. Transformer-Only produces spatially inconsistent results, lacking the local feature extraction necessary for precise boundary localization. Our model successfully balances spatial smoothness with detail preservation, accurately capturing gradual snow cover transitions and complex topographic effects. The spatially coherent uncertainty maps provide operationally valuable confidence information, with elevated uncertainty at ambiguous boundaries and low uncertainty in homogeneous regions. The model generalizes well to diverse test scenarios including forest edges, water bodies, and urban areas.

\subsection{Test-Time Augmentation and Ensemble Robustness}

\subsubsection{TTA Performance Enhancement}

Test-time augmentation (TTA) implementation significantly enhances prediction reliability across all test samples. Eight-fold geometric augmentation (rotation, reflection) improves accuracy by 2.3\% on average while reducing uncertainty by 22.4\%. This ensemble approach provides robust prediction intervals with accuracy gains ranging from 1.5\% to 3.0\% across different samples, confirming the value of TTA for operational snow monitoring applications.

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{images//results/tta_analysis_s00.png}
    \caption{Test-time augmentation analysis. Eight-fold geometric augmentation (rotation, reflection) improves accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%, demonstrating enhanced prediction reliability through ensemble averaging.}
    \label{fig:tta_analysis}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Ensemble Variance Reduction}

Ensemble variance analysis demonstrates successful uncertainty reduction through augmentation averaging. The 22.4\% reduction in uncertainty variance indicates that TTA effectively captures prediction variability arising from orientation-dependent features, while maintaining prediction accuracy. This enhancement validates the importance of rotational and reflectional invariance in snow cover mapping applications.

\subsection{Computational Efficiency}

\textbf{Model Complexity.} Our hybrid architecture contains 47.1M parameters, compared to 12.8M (Early Fusion), 26.2M (CNN-Only), and 21.3M (Transformer-Only). Despite increased complexity, the performance gains justify the additional parameters: 38.7\% MAE reduction over CNN-Only represents substantial practical improvement for operational snow monitoring. Training completed in 34.6 hours on a single NVIDIA A30 GPU with batch size 8, demonstrating feasibility for standard research infrastructure.

\textbf{Inference Speed.} The model processes 16$\times$16 patches (960m$\times$960m ground coverage at 60m resolution) with $\sim$50ms inference time per patch on A100 GPU. For the entire test set (18,117 patches), inference requires $\sim$15 minutes. Batch processing with batch size 32 achieves throughput of $\sim$2,000 patches/minute, enabling rapid large-scale deployment. Peak GPU memory consumption is 4.2GB during inference, allowing deployment on consumer-grade hardware.

\textbf{Scalability.} For full satellite tile processing, sliding window extraction with configurable stride enables trade-off between spatial coverage and computational cost. The model's multi-resolution design naturally handles varying input sizes, supporting operational deployment at continental scales. Test-time augmentation increases inference time by 8$\times$ but provides significant accuracy and uncertainty improvements, making it suitable for high-stakes operational applications where prediction reliability is critical.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}

\textbf{Dataset Scope.} Our test set covers 4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. Future work should evaluate on multi-season, multi-region datasets to assess year-round operational readiness and transferability to different snow conditions (maritime vs. continental snow, high-altitude vs. lowland environments).

\textbf{Comparison Scope.} We compare against three baseline architectures trained on identical data and reference published methods where metrics are available. Comprehensive comparison with operational snow products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F, Sentinel-3 SLSTR) and additional fusion architectures remains future work. Our focus on architectural innovations and systematic ablation studies aligns with demonstrating methodological contributions, but operational validation requires direct comparison with existing operational systems.

\textbf{Application Domain.} While we demonstrate effectiveness for fractional snow cover mapping, our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture with cross-modal attention, multi-resolution processing, and quality-aware learning should transfer to other remote sensing tasks (land cover classification, change detection, crop monitoring, disaster assessment). Evaluating task transferability and domain adaptation represents promising future research directions.

