\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:proposed_method}

\subsection{Overview}

Our methodology addresses the challenge of high-resolution fractional snow cover estimation through a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture that fuses Sentinel-1 SAR (10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 bands unified at 10m). The framework integrates three principal innovations: multi-resolution processing that enables training at 60m resolution while producing 10m predictions, cross-attention fusion that explicitly models inter-sensor dependencies, and quality-aware learning that leverages auxiliary metadata for robust training under heterogeneous data conditions. Figure~\ref{fig:overall_methodology} illustrates the complete processing pipeline from satellite data acquisition through preprocessing, multi-modal fusion, and high-resolution prediction.

\added{\textbf{Distinction from Prior Work.} While SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution training have been explored independently in remote sensing literature, existing approaches face specific limitations that our methodology addresses. First, conventional fusion strategies (concatenation, late fusion, attention-weighted averaging) treat modalities symmetrically without explicitly modeling \textit{how} information from one sensor should inform interpretation of the other; our bidirectional cross-attention enables SAR features to selectively query relevant optical context and vice versa through learned query-key-value projections (Section~\ref{sec:cross_attention}). Second, multi-resolution approaches typically employ fixed aggregation (average/max pooling) that ignores scene content; our adaptive aggregation learns to preserve snow-relevant information through content-aware pooling decisions (Section~\ref{sec:multi_resolution}). Third, existing quality-aware methods often use binary masks or single-source quality indicators; our framework integrates heterogeneous metadata from multiple sources (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) through multiplicative weighting with curriculum scheduling (Section~\ref{sec:quality_aware}). The novelty lies not in the individual concepts but in their principled integration within a unified architecture designed for the specific challenges of operational satellite-based snow monitoring.}

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/overall_methodology.png}
    \caption{The model consists of dual-path CNN backbones with modality-specific attention (CBAM for Sentinel-1 SAR, ECA for Sentinel-2 optical) that extract multi-scale   features through four stages (64, 128, 256, 512 channels). Shallow Feature Fusion (SFF) modules merge  cross-modal information at each scale, with skip connections (orange dashed arrows) propagating  multi-resolution features to the decoder. Three novel contributions are highlighted: (1) Multi-Resolution  Processing enables training on coarse 60m labels while processing fine 10-20m inputs through learned  adaptive aggregation and guided disaggregation, (2) the Fusion Vision Transformer (FVit) employs learnable  adaptive fusion weights ($\lambda_{sa}$, $\lambda_{ca}$) in its Ada-MBA stage to dynamically balance  self-attention and cross-attention based on data quality, and (3) Quality-Aware Training incorporates cloud   masks and quality flags through confidence-weighted loss with curriculum learning. The decoder produces  dual outputs: fractional snow cover prediction $\hat{y}$ and uncertainty estimate $\sigma^2$.}
    \label{fig:overall_methodology}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Data Preparation.} Sentinel-1 Ground Range Detected (GRD) products undergo standard SAR preprocessing including radiometric calibration to sigma-nought backscatter, Enhanced Lee Sigma speckle filtering (7$\times$7 window), and Range-Doppler orthorectification using SRTM 30m DEM \cite{kaushik2018comparative}. Sentinel-2 Level-1C products are atmospherically corrected using a hybrid Dark Object Subtraction and MODTRAN-based approach, incorporating Rayleigh scattering, aerosol transmission (AOT at 550nm), and water vapor absorption corrections \cite{sola2018assessment}. Both modalities are geometrically co-registered through bilinear resampling to a common 10m grid aligned with UTM coordinates. Temporal pairing selects Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 acquisitions within 3 days using Gaussian temporal weighting ($\sigma_t = 1.5$ days) to minimize snow condition changes between observations.

Snow-specific spectral indices are computed from Sentinel-2 bands to provide auxiliary features: the Normalized Difference Snow Index NDSI $= (\rho_{B3} - \rho_{B11})/(\rho_{B3} + \rho_{B11})$ exploits snow's high visible and low SWIR reflectance, Enhanced NDSI incorporates NDVI correction to reduce vegetation interference, and the Snow Cover Area Index combines NIR-SWIR differences with vegetation masking \cite{voitik2023comparison}. Quality metadata from Sentinel-2 Scene Classification Layer (SCL), cloud probability masks, and GFSC quality control layers (QC, QCFLAGS) provide spatially explicit data quality indicators used during training and inference.

\subsection{Multi-Resolution Processing Framework}
\label{sec:multi_resolution}

The multi-resolution framework addresses a fundamental challenge in operational satellite monitoring: high-resolution sensors (Sentinel-1/2 at 10m) lack corresponding high-resolution reference data, with widely-available products like GFSC providing only 60m resolution. Traditional approaches either train at coarse resolution (sacrificing input detail) or directly upscale labels (introducing interpolation artifacts). Our approach operates at three resolution stages: high-resolution input fusion at 10m, adaptive aggregation to 60m for supervised training, and guided disaggregation back to 10m for enhanced prediction (Figure~\ref{fig:multi_resolution_schematic}).

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/Multiresoulution_processing_framwork.png}
    \caption{Multi-resolution processing framework. The novel training strategy fuses Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data at 10m resolution, aggregates features to 60m for supervised training against GFSC reference data with quality-aware loss weighting, and disaggregates predictions back to 10m through guided upsampling that preserves fine-scale spatial detail.}
    \label{fig:multi_resolution_schematic}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Resolution Aggregation ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m}$).} During training, high-resolution features are aggregated to match the 60m resolution of GFSC reference data through adaptive pooling that preserves snow-relevant information. \added{Unlike fixed pooling operations (average or max) that apply uniformly regardless of scene content, our aggregation adapts based on learned snow probability---using max pooling in snow-dominated regions to preserve peak signals and average pooling elsewhere to maintain spatial consistency. This content-aware design prevents the information loss that occurs when fixed aggregation averages out fine-scale snow boundaries.} The aggregation function employs snow-aware weighting:

\begin{equation}
A_{adaptive}(X_{10m}) =
\begin{cases}
\max_{(i,j) \in W_{6\times6}} X_{10m}(i,j) & \text{if } P_{snow}(W_{6\times6}) > \tau_{snow} \\
\frac{1}{36} \sum_{(i,j) \in W_{6\times6}} X_{10m}(i,j) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

where $W_{6\times6}$ represents the 6$\times$6 aggregation window corresponding to the 6:1 resolution ratio, and $P_{snow}$ is the learned snow probability within the window. This adaptive strategy employs max pooling for snow-dominated regions (preserving peak snow signals) and average pooling elsewhere (maintaining spatial consistency). The snow probability is computed through a lightweight detection network: $P_{snow}(W) = \frac{1}{|W|} \sum_{(i,j) \in W} \sigma(f_{snow}(X_{10m}(i,j)))$, where $\sigma$ is sigmoid activation and $f_{snow}$ is a learned detection function with threshold $\tau_{snow} = 0.7$.

\textbf{Guided Disaggregation ($60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$).} During inference, coarse-resolution predictions are disaggregated to 10m through guided filtering that leverages high-resolution input features to recover fine-scale spatial detail. The disaggregation employs a modified guided filter framework \cite{he2012guided}:

\begin{equation}
\hat{X}_{10m} = a \cdot G_{10m} + b
\end{equation}

where $G_{10m}$ is the high-resolution guidance image derived from input Sentinel-1/2 features, and coefficients $a$ and $b$ are computed through local linear regression:

\begin{equation}
a = \frac{\text{Cov}(G, X)}{\text{Var}(G) + \epsilon}, \quad b = \mu_X - a \cdot \mu_G
\end{equation}

Here $\mu_G$ and $\mu_X$ are local means of guidance and upsampled data, computed over sliding windows. The regularization parameter $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ prevents division by zero in homogeneous regions. This guided approach preserves edge information from high-resolution inputs while maintaining consistency with coarse-resolution predictions, enabling the model to generate enhanced 10m outputs that capture sub-60m spatial patterns absent in training targets.

\textbf{Multi-Scale Loss Function.} Training incorporates multi-scale supervision to ensure consistency across resolution levels:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{multi\text{-}scale} = \sum_{k=0}^{3} \lambda_k \cdot \mathcal{L}_{MSE}(\hat{Y}_k, Y_k)
\end{equation}

where $\lambda_k = 2^{-k}$ provides higher weight to finer scales, $\hat{Y}_k$ and $Y_k$ are predictions and targets at scale $k$, with $k=0$ corresponding to 10m resolution. This multi-scale formulation encourages the model to maintain spatial coherence across the resolution hierarchy while focusing primary supervision at the target 60m scale.

\subsection{Hybrid CNN-Transformer Architecture}
\label{sec:cross_attention}

The hybrid architecture synergistically combines convolutional neural networks for local feature extraction with vision transformers for global context modeling, specifically designed for multi-modal satellite data fusion. The architecture comprises four interconnected components: dual-path CNN backbones for modality-specific processing, cross-attention fusion for intelligent multi-modal integration, spatial transformers for landscape-scale dependency modeling, and feature pyramid networks for multi-scale representation.

\textbf{Dual-Path CNN Backbones.} Separate CNN pathways process Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data through structurally similar but parameter-independent networks, acknowledging fundamental differences between SAR backscatter and optical reflectance. Each pathway follows a ResNet-inspired architecture with four progressive stages featuring channel dimensions [64, 128, 256, 512] and spatial downsampling factors [4, 8, 16, 32]. Convolutional blocks incorporate group normalization (32 groups) to handle varying batch sizes, GELU activation for smooth gradients preserving subtle snow boundaries, and modality-specific attention mechanisms. Sentinel-1 features employ Convolutional Block Attention Modules (CBAM) to enhance both channel and spatial attention for SAR texture patterns, while Sentinel-2 features utilize Efficient Channel Attention (ECA) to emphasize spectral relationships critical for optical snow detection \cite{woo2018cbam, wang2020eca}.

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/cnn_backbone_arch.png}
    \caption{Dual-path CNN backbone architecture showing separate processing pathways for Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data, with modality-specific attention mechanisms and progressive feature extraction through four hierarchical stages.}
    \label{fig:cnn_backbone}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Cross-Attention Fusion Mechanism.} Multi-modal fusion employs bidirectional cross-attention to explicitly model inter-sensor dependencies. \added{Unlike early fusion (concatenation) that treats modalities as additional channels, or late fusion (ensemble) that combines independent predictions, cross-attention enables \textit{selective information exchange}: each modality learns to query task-relevant features from the other. This is particularly important for SAR-optical fusion where the relationship is asymmetric---SAR provides structural/textural information useful when optical data is cloud-contaminated, while optical spectral signatures disambiguate SAR backscatter variations caused by moisture, roughness, or snow wetness.} For features $\mathbf{F}_{S1} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C_1 \times H \times W}$ from Sentinel-1 and $\mathbf{F}_{S2} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C_2 \times H \times W}$ from Sentinel-2, the mechanism computes query-key-value attention in both directions:

\begin{equation}
\text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V}) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{K}^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)\mathbf{V}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{F}_{S1 \rightarrow S2} = \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}_{S1}, \mathbf{K}_{S2}, \mathbf{V}_{S2}), \quad \mathbf{F}_{S2 \rightarrow S1} = \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}_{S2}, \mathbf{K}_{S1}, \mathbf{V}_{S1})
\end{equation}

where queries, keys, and values are derived through learned linear projections. The fused representation combines both directional flows: $\mathbf{F}_{fused} = \text{LayerNorm}(\text{Linear}([\mathbf{F}_{S1 \rightarrow S2}; \mathbf{F}_{S2 \rightarrow S1}]))$. The cross-attention employs 8 attention heads with dimension $d_k = 64$, enabling the model to capture different aspects of cross-modal relationships simultaneously, texture correlations, intensity relationships, and spatial pattern correspondences between SAR and optical signatures.

\begin{figure}[!h]
      \centering

  \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/cross_attention_fusion.png}
      \caption{Cross-attention fusion mechanism showing bidirectional query-key-value attention between Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 features. The mechanism computes S1→S2 and S2→S1 attention maps using 8 parallel heads with dimension $d_k=64$, enabling each modality to query relevant information from the other for intelligent multi-modal integration.}
      \label{fig:cross_attention_fusion}
  \end{figure}

\textbf{Spatial Transformer.} The transformer component addresses convolutional limitations in capturing long-range spatial dependencies essential for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns influenced by topography and regional climate. The spatial self-attention mechanism preserves 2D topology through learnable relative position encoding:

\begin{equation}
\text{SpatialAttention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V})_{i,j} = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{q}_i \mathbf{k}_j^T}{\sqrt{d_k}} + \mathbf{B}_{i,j}\right)\mathbf{v}_j
\end{equation}

where $\mathbf{B}_{i,j} = \mathbf{W}_B[\text{rel\_pos}(i, j)]$ represents learnable relative position bias capturing spatial relationships between pixels. This formulation enables the model to learn that nearby pixels have stronger influence while allowing long-range interactions when topographically or climatically relevant. The transformer architecture employs 6 sequential blocks with 8 attention heads and 512 embedding dimensions, providing sufficient capacity for complex spatial relationships while operating on compact feature representations (H/32$\times$W/32) to maintain computational efficiency.

\textbf{Feature Pyramid Network.} Multi-scale processing through FPN addresses snow cover's inherent scale variability, from pixel-level presence to landscape-level distribution trends. The FPN incorporates adaptive scale selection through attention-weighted fusion:

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{F}_{pyramid}^{(l)} = \mathbf{A}^{(l)} \odot \mathbf{L}^{(l)} + (1 - \mathbf{A}^{(l)}) \odot \text{Upsample}(\mathbf{F}_{pyramid}^{(l+1)})
\end{equation}

where $\mathbf{A}^{(l)} = \sigma(\text{Conv}_{1 \times 1}(\text{GAP}(\mathbf{L}^{(l)})))$ represents learned attention weights determining optimal spatial scale for different scenarios. The FPN processes features at four pyramid levels (P2, P3, P4, P5) with 256-channel feature maps, progressively incorporating finer-scale information through bilinear upsampling and attention-weighted fusion from coarsest (P5) to finest (P2) levels.

\subsection{Quality-Aware Learning Framework}
\label{sec:quality_aware}

The quality-aware framework addresses challenges of training on heterogeneous satellite data with spatially and temporally varying quality through adaptive loss weighting, curriculum learning, and uncertainty quantification.

\added{\textbf{Distinction from Standard Training.} Conventional deep learning treats all training samples equally, assuming homogeneous data quality. This assumption is systematically violated in satellite imagery where cloud contamination, atmospheric interference, sensor artifacts, and reference data uncertainty vary spatially and temporally. Simple binary masking (excluding low-quality pixels) discards potentially useful information, while equal weighting allows noisy samples to corrupt learned representations. Our approach instead modulates the contribution of each pixel through continuous quality weights derived from multiple independent metadata sources, combined with curriculum scheduling that progressively introduces challenging samples as the model develops robustness.}

\textbf{Confidence-Weighted Training.} Quality information from multiple sources---Sentinel-2 SCL flags, cloud probability masks, GFSC QC layers, terrain complexity indices---is integrated through multiplicative loss weighting:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{quality} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i^{conf} \cdot w_i^{qual} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{base}(y_i, \hat{y}_i)
\end{equation}

where $w_i^{conf} = \sigma(c_i)^\alpha$ represents confidence weight from model predictions with strength parameter $\alpha$, and $w_i^{qual} = \prod_{j=1}^{K} q_{i,j}^{\beta_j}$ denotes quality mask weight from auxiliary indicators. This combined weighting emphasizes high-quality samples while maintaining gradient flow for challenging cases, enabling the model to learn robust representations without requiring extensive manual data curation.

\textbf{Curriculum Learning.} Training progresses through a curriculum that transitions from high-confidence to challenging samples via exponential decay of confidence thresholds:

\begin{equation}
\tau(e) = \max(\tau_{init} \cdot \exp(-\lambda \cdot p), \tau_{final})
\end{equation}

where $\tau(e)$ is the threshold at epoch $e$, $p = e/E_{total}$ represents progress, $\tau_{init} = 0.9$ and $\tau_{final} = 0.3$ define initial and final thresholds, and $\lambda = 3$ controls decay rate. This strategy ensures stable early learning on high-quality data while gradually exposing the model to degraded conditions representative of operational scenarios.

\begin{figure}[!h]
      \centering

  \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/quality_aware_training.png}
      \caption{Quality-aware training framework integrating multi-source  metadata (GFSC QC, Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT/WVP) through multiplicative loss  weighting. Curriculum learning progressively transitions from high-confidence samples ($\tau=0.9$) to challenging degraded conditions  ($\tau=0.3$) via exponential decay. The uncertainty quantification head  provides well-calibrated total uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) with physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$).}
      \label{fig:quality_aware_training}
  \end{figure}

\textbf{Uncertainty Quantification.} The framework provides pixel-wise uncertainty estimates through a dedicated prediction head that outputs total uncertainty reflecting both data quality and model confidence. Unlike approaches that decompose aleatoric and epistemic uncertainties through specialized architectures or Monte Carlo sampling, our implementation focuses on total uncertainty for operational efficiency while maintaining excellent calibration properties (ECE=0.025). The uncertainty head employs softplus activation to ensure positive values: $\sigma^2 = \log(1 + \exp(\mathbf{z}))$ where $\mathbf{z}$ is the raw network output. Uncertainty estimates are regularized through negative log-likelihood loss encouraging higher uncertainty for larger prediction errors:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{uncertainty} = -\frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^2) - \frac{(\mathbf{y} - \hat{\mathbf{y}})^2}{2\sigma^2}
\end{equation}

This formulation provides well-calibrated confidence estimates that enable targeted quality control and risk-informed decision-making in downstream applications.

\textbf{Complete Loss Function.} The total training objective integrates multiple components addressing prediction accuracy, spatial consistency, and uncertainty calibration:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{total} = \alpha \mathcal{L}_{regression} + \beta \mathcal{L}_{perceptual} + \gamma \mathcal{L}_{consistency} + \delta \mathcal{L}_{uncertainty}
\end{equation}

The primary regression loss employs smooth L1 formulation with quality weighting: $\mathcal{L}_{regression} = \frac{1}{|\Omega|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \Omega} w_{i,j} \cdot \text{SmoothL1}(y_{i,j}, \hat{y}_{i,j})$ where $\Omega$ represents valid pixels. 

Perceptual consistency enforces multi-scale feature-level similarity through VGG-based perceptual loss \cite{johnson2016perceptual}: $\mathcal{L}_{perceptual} = \sum_{l=1}^{L} \lambda_l \|\Phi_l(\mathbf{y}) - \Phi_l(\hat{\mathbf{y}})\|_2^2$. 

Spatial consistency maintains local coherence via gradient matching: $\mathcal{L}_{consistency} = \|\nabla \mathbf{y} - \nabla \hat{\mathbf{y}}\|_1$. 

Loss component weights $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta)$ are learned through gradient-based optimization rather than manual tuning, enabling adaptive balancing throughout training.

\subsection{Training and Implementation}

\textbf{Training Protocol.} The model is trained end-to-end using AdamW optimizer \cite{loshchilov2017decoupled} with weight decay $1 \times 10^{-4}$ and effective batch size 64 achieved through gradient accumulation. Learning rate follows cosine annealing with warm restarts, initializing at $1 \times 10^{-3}$ and decaying to $1 \times 10^{-5}$ over 100 epochs. Mixed-precision training (FP16) with dynamic loss scaling reduces memory requirements by approximately 40\% while maintaining numerical stability. Regularization includes dropout (0.1), stochastic depth (0.1), and label smoothing (0.1) to improve generalization. Training employs random cropping to 100$\times$100 patches, horizontal and vertical flipping, random rotation ($\pm$15$^\circ$), and Gaussian noise augmentation ($\sigma = 0.01$) to enhance robustness.

\textbf{Computational Efficiency.} The architecture balances accuracy with computational feasibility through strategic design choices. Grouped normalization reduces parameters compared to instance normalization, hierarchical CNN structure minimizes complexity via progressive downsampling, and transformer operation on compact representations (H/32$\times$W/32) significantly reduces quadratic self-attention complexity. Gradient checkpointing for transformer layers trades computation for memory savings. The complete architecture processes 100$\times$100 patches with approximately 45M parameters and 12 GFLOPs, achieving inference speeds suitable for operational applications while enabling training on standard GPU hardware (32GB memory).

\textbf{Inference Strategy.} For images exceeding 100$\times$100 pixels, inference employs sliding window prediction with 64-pixel overlap. Overlapping regions are blended using Gaussian weights to eliminate boundary artifacts, ensuring spatially smooth predictions across patch boundaries. Test-time augmentation with 8-fold geometric transformations (rotations and reflections) further enhances prediction reliability, with ensemble averaging improving accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Interpretation of Key Results}

The hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal satellite fusion achieved state-of-the-art performance across all evaluation metrics. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy on 18,117 test patches from 4 geographically separated tiles, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). These accuracy levels substantially exceed established operational requirements for snow water equivalent modeling, where estimation errors below 15-20\% are generally acceptable for hydrological forecasting applications \cite{hall2002modis, gascoin2019theia}, achieving approximately 5\% residual error rate (100\% - 94.66\%) suitable for critical operational deployments. The correlation coefficient of $r=0.784$ ($p<0.001$) between predictions and GFSC reference data demonstrates strong model calibration and predictive accuracy for continuous fractional snow cover estimation. This correlation strength ($R^2=0.588$) is particularly significant given that the model operates at 10m resolution while training on 60m reference data, indicating successful multi-resolution knowledge transfer. The combination of high correlation, substantial explained variance (58.8\%), and low error metrics (MAE: 13.77\%, RMSE: 0.180) confirms precise quantitative predictions suitable for operational applications requiring fine-grained snow cover fraction estimates.

Critically, the uncertainty quantification framework demonstrates excellent calibration properties with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025, well below the 0.03 threshold for well-calibrated models. Mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matches the observed $\sim$10\% error rate (100\% - 89.52\% accuracy during validation), confirming that the model's confidence estimates accurately reflect prediction reliability. Spatially coherent uncertainty patterns exhibit elevated uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries (Moran's I = 0.548, p = 0.002), demonstrating physically realistic uncertainty estimation with moderate spatial autocorrelation that aligns with known sources of ambiguity in remote sensing-based snow detection. These well-calibrated uncertainty estimates provide decision-makers with trustworthy quantitative confidence bounds for applications ranging from avalanche risk assessment to water resource planning.

\subsection{Baseline Comparison Insights}

The systematic comparison with strong baseline architectures validates each key design choice in our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture. Early Fusion (simple concatenation) achieves only 83.98\% accuracy, demonstrating that treating heterogeneous modalities as additional channels fails to capture the complex, non-linear relationships between SAR backscatter and optical spectral signatures. CNN-Only improves to 89.13\% accuracy by employing modality-specific feature extraction, but still falls 5.5 percentage points short of our hybrid model, indicating that pure CNN architectures lack the global context modeling necessary for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns influenced by topography and regional climate. Transformer-Only achieves only 85.35\% accuracy, underperforming even Early Fusion, which validates that local feature extraction through CNNs is essential for capturing fine-scale spatial patterns and precise boundary localization in fractional snow cover estimation.

The substantial performance gap between our hybrid architecture (94.66\% accuracy) and the best individual component (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) demonstrates synergistic benefits rather than simple additive improvements. The 5.5 percentage point accuracy gain represents a 48.7\% reduction in error rate (10.87\% $\rightarrow$ 5.34\%), confirming that CNNs and Transformers provide genuinely complementary capabilities: CNNs excel at extracting hierarchical spatial features and local texture patterns, while Transformers capture long-range dependencies and global context essential for understanding spatially distributed snow cover influenced by elevation gradients, aspect variations, and regional meteorological patterns. The ablation studies further validate this conclusion, showing that removing either cross-attention (5.16 point drop) or the transformer (3.86 point drop) causes substantial performance degradation, while removing both (reverting to Early Fusion baseline) results in catastrophic 10.68 point accuracy loss.

Statistical quality metrics further demonstrate the hybrid architecture's superiority. Our model achieves $R^2=0.588$ (58.8\% explained variance), substantially exceeding CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), and Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). The 24.9 percentage point improvement in explained variance over CNN-Only ($R^2$: 0.239 $\rightarrow$ 0.588) represents 2.5$\times$ better performance, confirming that the hybrid design captures substantially more of the underlying snow cover variance through its combination of local feature extraction and global context modeling.

\subsection{Multi-Resolution Processing Effectiveness}

  \begin{figure}[!h]
      \centering
      \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/multi_resolution_schematic.png}
      \caption{Multi-resolution processing framework schematic. The novel training strategy operates at three resolution stages: (1) high-resolution input fusion at 10m combining Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data through hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture, (2) adaptive aggregation to 60m for supervised training against GFSC  reference data with quality-aware loss weighting, and (3) guided disaggregation back to 10m for enhanced spatial detail prediction during   inference. This approach enables training on widely-available coarse-resolution labels while producing high-resolution outputs that preserve fine-scale spatial patterns absent in training targets.}
      \label{fig:multi_resolution_schematic}
  \end{figure}

The novel $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy represents a significant methodological advancement that addresses fundamental limitations in earth observation where high-resolution sensors lack corresponding high-resolution reference data. As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:multi_resolution_schematic}, the multi-resolution framework enabled the model to leverage fine-scale spatial information from Sentinel-1 (10m) and Sentinel-2 (10m unified resolution) while training against coarser GFSC reference data (60m), subsequently producing enhanced 10m predictions that preserve spatial detail absent in the training targets. The effectiveness of this approach is evidenced by the model's ability to generate spatially coherent high-resolution outputs that maintain consistency with coarse-resolution supervision while achieving excellent performance metrics (accuracy $>$89\%, correlation r=0.881). The guided disaggregation mechanism, employing spatial correlation modeling and bilateral upsampling, successfully recovered fine-scale snow cover patterns that would be undetectable through simple interpolation of 60m reference data. The model's consistent performance across diverse snow coverage scenarios (0.2\%-75.5\%) confirms its capacity to reconstruct sub-pixel detail guided by high-resolution input features.

This multi-resolution strategy offers several advantages over traditional single-resolution approaches. First, it enables training on widely available coarse-resolution reference GFSC data while still producing high-resolution outputs that exploit the full spatial capacity of modern satellite sensors. Second, the learned aggregation functions preserve critical spatial information during downsampling, avoiding information loss inherent in fixed aggregation methods like simple averaging. Third, the approach is generalizable to other earth observation applications facing similar resolution mismatches between high-resolution input sensors and coarse-resolution reference data. The trade-offs of this approach merit consideration. Training on coarse-resolution targets may limit the model's ability to capture sub-60m snow cover features that lack representation in the training signal. However, the incorporation of high-resolution auxiliary features (NDSI, backscatter textures) and the guided disaggregation framework partially mitigate this limitation by providing fine-scale structural information to guide the upsampling process. Future work could explore hybrid training strategies that combine coarse-resolution reference data with sparse high-resolution annotations to further enhance sub-pixel prediction capabilities.


\subsection{Cross-Modal Fusion Performance}

The architecture's multi-modal fusion design combines Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data to achieve excellent performance (accuracy: 89.52\%, F1-score: 90.85\%), leveraging the complementary information captured by SAR and optical modalities. SAR provides all-weather capability with sensitivity to surface roughness and moisture, while optical sensors offer rich spectral information for snow discrimination. The achieved performance demonstrates that intelligent fusion of these modalities enables accurate snow cover estimation across diverse conditions. The cross-attention fusion mechanism enables explicit modeling of bidirectional dependencies between SAR backscatter patterns and optical spectral signatures. The attention mechanism allows each modality to dynamically weight the relevance of information from the complementary modality, adapting to varying data quality and snow conditions. This design choice supports the model's robust performance across the full range of snow coverage scenarios tested (0.2\%-75.5\%), from near-snow-free conditions where optical information dominates to cloud-contaminated scenarios where SAR provides critical all-weather observations. The model's consistent performance across varying atmospheric conditions validates the fusion architecture's effectiveness at leveraging complementary sensor information. The quality-aware training framework further enhances fusion robustness by appropriately weighting samples based on data quality metadata, enabling the model to learn optimal sensor utilization patterns under diverse observation conditions.


\subsection{Quality-Aware Training Impact}

The quality-aware training framework contributes to the model's excellent overall performance (accuracy: 89.52\%, F1-score: 90.85\%) by enabling robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data with varying quality characteristics. Explicit incorporation of data quality information through multiplicative loss weighting enables the model to learn representations that generalize effectively across challenging atmospheric conditions and varying snow coverage scenarios (0.2\%-75.5\%). The integration of auxiliary metadata from multiple sources (Sentinel-2 AOT, WVP, SCL; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) provided complementary quality indicators that collectively enhanced training efficiency. The adaptive weighting scheme $\text{weight} = (\text{confidence}^\alpha) \times (\text{quality\_mask}^\beta)$ effectively balanced sample diversity with quality emphasis, avoiding the pitfall of training exclusively on high-quality samples while still prioritizing reliable data.

The curriculum learning component, progressively relaxing confidence thresholds from $\tau_{init}=0.9$ to $\tau_{final}=0.3$, proved particularly valuable for handling the heterogeneous quality characteristics of satellite data. This progressive strategy enabled stable learning dynamics during early training while gradually exposing the model to more challenging scenarios, ultimately improving robustness to edge cases and degraded data conditions. The operational implications of quality-aware training are significant. Models trained with quality awareness demonstrate superior performance under real-world conditions where data quality varies spatially and temporally. The uncertainty estimates enable operational systems to automatically flag low-confidence predictions, reducing the risk of erroneous decisions in critical applications. Furthermore, the quality-aware framework facilitates efficient utilization of large but noisy satellite archives by extracting maximum value from available data rather than requiring extensive manual curation.

Limitations of the current quality-aware approach include reliance on pre-defined quality indicators that may not capture all sources of data degradation. Future work could explore learned quality assessment networks that automatically identify degraded data based on input characteristics, potentially improving robustness to unanticipated quality issues.


\subsection{Uncertainty Quantification and Reliability}

The uncertainty quantification framework provides operationally valuable insights into prediction reliability through spatially coherent uncertainty estimates. The framework successfully identifies regions where predictions are less reliable, with elevated uncertainty at snow-land boundaries and areas with complex topography, demonstrating physically realistic uncertainty estimation that aligns with known sources of ambiguity in snow detection. The operational value of uncertainty quantification extends beyond simple confidence flagging. Quantified uncertainties enable probabilistic decision-making frameworks where downstream applications can incorporate prediction uncertainty into risk assessments. For example, flood forecasting models could weight snow cover predictions by their uncertainty estimates, propagating uncertainty through hydrological models to generate probabilistic runoff predictions. Similarly, avalanche risk assessment systems could flag high-uncertainty regions for additional monitoring or ground validation.

Uncertainty analysis revealed that prediction confidence appropriately varies with data quality, surface complexity, and snow coverage characteristics. Elevated uncertainty in regions with limited training data representation (complex topography, rare surface types) or high atmospheric interference (cloud contamination, sensor noise) enables targeted quality control and adaptive processing strategies. This pattern enables targeted improvement strategies: increasing training data diversity for underrepresented scenarios and improving preprocessing algorithms to reduce uncertainty from atmospheric and sensor artifacts. The uncertainty framework's ability to appropriately increase estimates for out-of-distribution scenarios enhances operational reliability by flagging predictions that may require additional validation. Cross-domain validation confirmed effective separation of high-confidence from low-confidence predictions, reducing the risk of overconfident errors in atypical conditions.


\subsection{Limitations and Challenges}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dataset Scope.} Our test set covers 4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region through rigorous 70/15/15 train/val/test split with 30-day temporal separation. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. The test set's 18,117 patches from unseen tiles provide strong evidence for spatial transferability, but year-round operational readiness requires multi-season evaluation. Future work should assess performance in mid-latitude regions with sporadic snow cover, forested environments with canopy interference, maritime vs. continental snow regimes, and high-altitude vs. lowland environments.

    \item \textbf{Comparison Scope.} We compare against three strong baseline architectures (Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only) trained on identical data, demonstrating 38.7\% MAE reduction and 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement over the best baseline. We also reference published methods (HybridSN, CRformer, CVTNet, mSwinUNet) where metrics are available. However, direct comparison with operational snow products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F, Sentinel-3 SLSTR) and traditional NDSI thresholding remains future work. Our focus on architectural innovations and systematic ablation studies demonstrates methodological contributions, but operational validation requires benchmarking against existing operational systems under identical evaluation protocols.

    \item \textbf{Computational Requirements.} Our hybrid architecture contains 47.1M parameters and requires 4.2GB GPU memory during inference, processing 16$\times$16 patches in $\sim$50ms on A100 GPU. Training completed in 34.6 hours on a single NVIDIA A30 GPU, demonstrating feasibility for standard research infrastructure. However, continental or global-scale deployment at full temporal resolution (daily Sentinel-1/2 acquisitions) would require distributed computing infrastructure or model compression techniques (pruning, quantization, knowledge distillation) to achieve operational throughput. Test-time augmentation increases inference time by 8$\times$ but provides significant reliability improvements, creating trade-offs between accuracy and computational efficiency that must be balanced based on application requirements.

    \item \textbf{Reference Data Limitations.} Training relies on Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) at 60m resolution as reference data, inheriting GFSC's own accuracy constraints. \added{Consequently, all quantitative metrics reported in this study (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, $R^2$) are computed at 60m resolution and represent consistency with the GFSC product rather than independently validated accuracy. The model's 10m prediction capability, achieved through guided disaggregation during inference, is validated qualitatively by visual alignment with high-resolution Sentinel-2 optical boundaries but lacks quantitative ground truth at sub-60m scales.} While our multi-resolution framework successfully leverages 10m input features through the $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy, fine-scale snow cover heterogeneity below 60m lacks direct supervision. The validation approach comparing predictions against GFSC may overestimate performance relative to independent ground truth. Future work should incorporate sparse high-resolution annotations from field surveys, airborne LiDAR, or UAV imagery to validate sub-60m prediction accuracy and provide complementary ground truth independent of GFSC.

    \item \textbf{Quality Metadata Dependency.} The quality-aware framework integrates auxiliary metadata (Sentinel-2 SCL, cloud masks, GFSC QC layers, atmospheric parameters) through multiplicative loss weighting. While this enhances training robustness, operational deployment requires consistent availability of quality information across all satellite acquisitions. Some older archive data or degraded processing scenarios may lack complete metadata. Robust fallback strategies (learned quality assessment, default weighting schemes) are essential for scenarios where quality information is incomplete or unreliable.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Broader Implications for Earth Observation}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The methodological contributions of this work extend beyond snow cover monitoring to broader earth observation applications facing similar challenges. The multi-resolution training paradigm addresses a fundamental problem in remote sensing where high-resolution sensors lack corresponding high-resolution reference data. This approach is applicable to diverse applications including land cover classification, vegetation monitoring, and urban change detection, where training labels are often available only at coarser resolutions than input sensors.

    \item The quality-aware learning framework provides a systematic approach for handling the heterogeneous data quality inherent in operational satellite archives. As earth observation moves toward big data analytics leveraging decades of historical observations, methods for learning from imperfect data while quantifying uncertainty become increasingly critical. The demonstrated success of quality-aware training with auxiliary metadata integration offers a template for other applications to leverage available quality information rather than requiring extensive manual data curation.

    \item The cross-modal fusion architecture demonstrates the value of explicit modeling of inter-sensor relationships through attention mechanisms. As multi-sensor earth observation systems become increasingly common (Sentinel constellation, Landsat-Sentinel integration, commercial radar-optical combinations), sophisticated fusion approaches that capture complementary information will become essential for extracting maximum value from available observations.

    \item The uncertainty quantification framework addresses a critical gap in operational earth observation systems. While machine learning methods increasingly achieve competitive accuracy, their adoption in operational contexts is limited by lack of reliability indicators. The demonstrated calibration of uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) with spatially coherent patterns provides a pathway for responsible deployment of machine learning in critical earth observation applications.

    \item The hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture showcases the synergistic combination of inductive biases (CNNs for local feature extraction) with global modeling capabilities (Transformers for long-range dependencies). This architectural paradigm is increasingly relevant for earth observation applications where both fine-scale spatial detail and landscape-scale context are essential for accurate predictions. The demonstrated computational efficiency of the hybrid approach, operating on compact feature representations ($H/32 \times W/32$), addresses scalability concerns while maintaining modeling capacity.

    \item The broader implications extend to policy and decision-making contexts where earth observation data informs critical decisions. The quantified uncertainties enable risk-informed decision-making frameworks that explicitly account for observation uncertainty. The all-weather monitoring capability, combining SAR and optical observations, addresses operational requirements for continuous environmental monitoring independent of atmospheric conditions. These capabilities advance toward the vision of operational, reliable earth observation systems that support evidence-based environmental management and climate adaptation strategies.
\end{enumerate}

