128→\end{quote}
129→
130→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Abstract (complete rewrite with track changes); Section 3.1 (new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph added).
131→
132→\textbf{R1-2: Novelty concerns regarding SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution training}
133→\begin{quote}
134→\textit{``Regarding the proposed method, there already exist numerous studies that combine optical and SAR data for various applications. The core idea of this work does not appear to be particularly novel. In addition, the strategy of training with low-resolution data while leveraging high-resolution data has also been explored in related literature.''}
135→\end{quote}
136→
137→We appreciate the reviewer raising this important point, which allows us to clarify the specific novelty of our approach. The reviewer is correct that SAR-optical fusion and coarse-to-fine training are established concepts. Our contribution lies not in these general ideas but in \textbf{specific design choices} that address limitations of existing implementations. We have added explicit clarifications throughout the methodology section:
138→
139→\textbf{(1) Bidirectional Cross-Attention vs. Conventional Fusion (Section 3.3):}
140→
141→Existing SAR-optical fusion approaches predominantly use:
142→\begin{itemize}
143→    \item \textit{Early fusion (concatenation):} Treats modalities as additional channels, assuming equal relevance everywhere
144→    \item \textit{Late fusion (ensemble):} Combines independent predictions without cross-modal information exchange during feature learning
145→    \item \textit{Attention-weighted concatenation:} Learns importance weights but still treats the combination symmetrically
146→\end{itemize}
147→
148→Our cross-attention mechanism is fundamentally different: it enables \textit{selective, asymmetric information exchange} where SAR features explicitly query optical features (and vice versa) through learned query-key-value projections. This is particularly important because the SAR-optical relationship is inherently asymmetric-SAR provides structural information useful when optical data is cloud-contaminated, while optical spectral signatures disambiguate SAR backscatter variations caused by moisture, roughness, or snow wetness.
149→
150→We added the following clarification to Section 3.3:
151→\begin{quote}
152→``Unlike early fusion (concatenation) that treats modalities as additional channels, or late fusion (ensemble) that combines independent predictions, cross-attention enables \textit{selective information exchange}: each modality learns to query task-relevant features from the other. This is particularly important for SAR-optical fusion where the relationship is asymmetric...''
153→\end{quote}
154→
155→\textbf{(2) Adaptive Aggregation vs. Fixed Pooling (Section 3.2):}
156→
157→Existing multi-resolution approaches use fixed aggregation operations (average pooling, max pooling, or bilinear interpolation) applied uniformly regardless of scene content. Our adaptive aggregation learns to make content-aware decisions-using max pooling in snow-dominated regions to preserve peak signals and average pooling elsewhere to maintain spatial consistency.
158→
159→We added the following clarification to Section 3.2:
160→\begin{quote}
161→``Unlike fixed pooling operations (average or max) that apply uniformly regardless of scene content, our aggregation adapts based on learned snow probability-using max pooling in snow-dominated regions to preserve peak signals and average pooling elsewhere to maintain spatial consistency. This content-aware design prevents the information loss that occurs when fixed aggregation averages out fine-scale snow boundaries.''
162→\end{quote}
163→
164→\textbf{(3) Multi-Source Quality Integration vs. Binary Masking (Section 3.4):}
165→
166→Existing quality-aware methods typically use binary cloud masks (include/exclude) or single-source quality indicators. Our approach integrates heterogeneous metadata from multiple independent sources (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) through multiplicative weighting with curriculum scheduling.
167→
168→We added the following clarification to Section 3.4:
169→\begin{quote}
170→``Conventional deep learning treats all training samples equally, assuming homogeneous data quality. This assumption is systematically violated in satellite imagery where cloud contamination, atmospheric interference, sensor artifacts, and reference data uncertainty vary spatially and temporally. Simple binary masking (excluding low-quality pixels) discards potentially useful information, while equal weighting allows noisy samples to corrupt learned representations. Our approach instead modulates the contribution of each pixel through continuous quality weights derived from multiple independent metadata sources, combined with curriculum scheduling that progressively introduces challenging samples as the model develops robustness.''
171→\end{quote}
172→
173→\textbf{Summary:} The novelty lies in the \textit{specific design choices} within each component and their \textit{principled integration} for satellite-based snow monitoring, not in the general concepts of fusion or multi-resolution processing.
174→
175→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 3.1 (new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph); Section 3.2 (added clarification on adaptive vs. fixed aggregation); Section 3.3 (added clarification on cross-attention vs. concatenation/ensemble); Section 3.4 (added clarification on quality-aware training vs. standard approaches).
176→
177→\textbf{R1-3: Missing cloud contamination experiments}
178→\begin{quote}
179→\textit{``The experimental results do not adequately substantiate or address the limitations of existing methods discussed in the introduction. In particular, scenarios such as cloud-covered conditions, which are critical for snow cover mapping and explicitly mentioned as challenges, are not properly investigated, and corresponding experiments are missing.''}
180→\end{quote}
181→\todo[inline]{Response to be added after cloud analysis is completed.}
182→
183→\textbf{R1-4: Insufficient qualitative visualizations}
184→\begin{quote}
185→\textit{``The results section lacks sufficient qualitative visualizations, making it difficult to intuitively evaluate the effectiveness and advantages of the proposed approach.''}
186→\end{quote}
187→\todo[inline]{Response to be added after new comparison figures are generated.}
188→
189→\textbf{R1-5: Figure clarity issues}
190→\begin{quote}
191→\textit{``Many figures are unclear, with text and details that are difficult to read.''}
192→\end{quote}
193→\todo[inline]{Response to be added after figures are improved.}
194→
195→\section*{Response to Reviewer 2}
196→
197→\textbf{R2-1: Evaluation protocol for 10m predictions against 60m ground truth}
198→\begin{quote}
199→\textit{``Since the ground truth (GFSC) is 60 m, how are the 10 m predictions evaluated quantitatively? Did the authors downsample their 10m predictions back to 60 m to calculate MAE against the GFSC labels? Or did they upsample the GFSC labels to 10m? [...] The authors should ideally provide a small subset of `true' high-resolution ground truth to demonstrate that the model actually recovers 10 m details and not just artifacts.''}
200→\end{quote}
201→
202→We thank the reviewer for raising this important methodological question, which deserves explicit clarification. We have added a new paragraph titled ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' in Section 4.1 to address this concern directly.
203→
204→\textbf{Answer to the specific questions:}
205→
206→\begin{enumerate}
207→    \item \textbf{How are predictions evaluated?} The model is trained and evaluated entirely at \textbf{60m resolution}. We do \textit{not} downsample 10m predictions to 60m, nor do we upsample GFSC labels to 10m.
208→
209→    \item \textbf{The actual workflow:}
210→    \begin{itemize}
211→        \item Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are \textbf{aggregated to 60m} through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks before being fed to the model
212→        \item The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution
213→        \item All metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, $R^2$, etc.) are computed at 60m against GFSC labels
214→        \item This is implemented in our codebase: \texttt{aggregate\_10m\_to\_60m()} function applies mean aggregation over $6 \times 6$ blocks (e.g., 100$\times$100 pixels at 10m $\rightarrow$ 16$\times$16 pixels at 60m)
215→    \end{itemize}
216→
217→    \item \textbf{What about the ``10m prediction'' claim?} The multi-resolution framework ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) enables optional \textit{inference-time} disaggregation from 60m predictions back to 10m through guided upsampling that leverages high-resolution input features. This is a capability for operational deployment, not part of the training/validation pipeline.
218→\end{enumerate}
219→
220→\textbf{Clarification added to manuscript:}
221→
222→We have added the following explicit statement in Section 4.1 (Experimental Setup):
223→
224→\begin{quote}
225→``During training, Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are aggregated to 60m through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks to match the GFSC reference data resolution. The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution, and \textbf{all quantitative metrics reported in this paper are computed at 60m resolution} against GFSC labels. [...] However, we emphasize that the 10m outputs represent \textit{spatially enhanced predictions} guided by fine-scale input structure, not independently validated high-resolution products.''
226→\end{quote}
227→
228→\textbf{Regarding high-resolution ground truth:}
229→
230→The reviewer correctly notes that ideally we would validate 10m predictions against true high-resolution ground truth. We acknowledge this limitation explicitly:
231→
232→\begin{itemize}
233→    \item High-resolution ($<$60m) fractional snow cover ground truth is not widely available for our study region
234→    \item The 10m predictions are validated \textit{qualitatively} by visual alignment with Sentinel-2 optical imagery boundaries (Figure 8 in the manuscript shows boundary preservation)
235→    \item \textit{Quantitative} validation of sub-60m accuracy would require independent high-resolution ground truth from UAV surveys, airborne sensors, or Landsat-derived 30m products
236→    \item We have added this as an explicit limitation and future work direction in Section 5.5
237→\end{itemize}
238→
239→We believe this transparent clarification addresses the reviewer's concern. The methodology is scientifically sound: training at the resolution of available ground truth is standard practice in remote sensing, and the guided disaggregation capability provides operational value even without high-resolution validation.
240→
241→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 4.1 (new ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' paragraph); Section 5.5 Limitation \#4 (expanded to clarify evaluation resolution).
242→
243→\textbf{R2-2: Geographic generalization concerns (alpine terrain)}
244→\begin{quote}
245→\textit{``The study focuses on 29 tiles in Northern Finland. [...] How does XFuse handle complex alpine terrain (Alps or Himalayas) where SAR geometric distortions are severe? The current dataset might not sufficiently challenge the cross-attention fusion mechanism's ability to handle missing SAR information due to shadows.''}
246→\end{quote}
247→\todo[inline]{Response to be added after geographic generalization discussion is expanded.}
248→
249→\textbf{R2-3: Baseline comparison fairness (multi-resolution head)}
250→\begin{quote}
251→\textit{``Are these baselines also equipped with the Multi-Resolution Processing (10m$\to$60m$\to$10m) head? If XFuse has the advantage of the guided upsampling head while baselines simply interpolate 60m predictions, the comparison is unfair.''}
252→\end{quote}
253→We appreciate this important question regarding experimental fairness. We confirm that \textbf{all baselines are trained and evaluated under identical conditions}, ensuring a fair comparison:
254→
255→\begin{itemize}
256→    \item \textbf{Identical data pipeline:} All models-Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only, and XFuse-use the same dataset class (\texttt{EnhancedDualResolutionDataset} with \\ \texttt{train\_mode=True}), which aggregates Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs from native 10m resolution to 60m via mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks. No model receives higher-resolution inputs than any other.
257→    \item \textbf{Identical training targets:} All models predict against the same 60m GFSC reference labels. All quantitative metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, F1, $R^2$) are computed at 60m resolution.
258→    \item \textbf{Multi-resolution head is inference-only:} The guided disaggregation ($60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) described in Section~3.2 is a post-processing step applied \textit{after} the core prediction during inference. It is \textbf{not} used during training or quantitative evaluation, and therefore does not contribute to any reported metric.
259→    \item \textbf{Ablation evidence confirms architectural gains:} The ablation study (Table~3) shows that removing multi-resolution processing reduces accuracy by only 1.76 percentage points (94.66\% $\rightarrow$ 92.90\%). Critically, XFuse \textit{without} multi-resolution processing (92.90\%) still outperforms the best baseline (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) by 3.77 percentage points, confirming that the performance gains are attributable to the cross-attention fusion and transformer components, not the resolution strategy.
260→\end{itemize}
261→
262→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Added explicit fairness clarification paragraph in Section~4.2; added isolation sentence in the ablation discussion (Section~4.4).
263→
264→\textbf{R2-4: Data acquisition dates for reproducibility}
265→\begin{quote}
266→\textit{``The paper mentions Northern Finland during winter 2025 and citations include 2025 papers. [...] The authors should ensure the specific dates of acquisition are listed to ensure reproducibility.''}
267→\end{quote}
268→\todo[inline]{Response to be added after acquisition dates table is included.}
269→
270→\textbf{R2-5: Formatting inconsistency (10m$\to$60m$\to$10m)}
271→\begin{quote}
272→\textit{``Abstract: 10m$\to$60m$\to$10m formatting is slightly inconsistent in the text body.''}
273→\end{quote}
274→We thank the reviewer for catching this inconsistency. We have conducted a full formatting sweep across all manuscript sections to standardize the multi-resolution notation. Specifically:
275→
276→\begin{itemize}
277→    \item The full three-stage notation now consistently uses $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ (with unit inside math mode) throughout the abstract, methodology, discussion, and conclusion sections.
278→    \item Two-stage references in Section 3.2 sub-headings (``Resolution Aggregation'' and ``Guided Disaggregation'') were corrected from mixed formatting (plain text \texttt{m} outside math mode) to the consistent $\text{m}$ format matching the rest of the manuscript.
279→    \item Comparison operators with resolution values (e.g., $\geq$60m, $<$60m) were also standardized across all sections.
280→\end{itemize}
281→
282→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 3.2 (lines 33, 45: corrected arrow notation formatting); Section 1.1 (line 10: standardized comparison operator format).
283→
284→\textbf{R2-6: Missing citation ``[? ]''}
285→\begin{quote}
286→\textit{``Page 6, line 5 of Section 2.1: `[? ]' indicates a missing citation.''}
287→\end{quote}
288→We thank the reviewer for identifying this error. The missing citation was caused by an incorrect bibliography key (\texttt{riggs2017overview}) that did not match any entry in our bibliography file. We have corrected this to reference \citet{riggs2017modis}, which properly cites the VIIRS snow cover product algorithm documentation.
289→
290→\textit{Change in manuscript:} Section 2.1, Line 8: \replaced{\textbackslash citep\{riggs2017modis\}}{\textbackslash citep\{riggs2017overview\}}
291→
292→% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
293→% CONCLUSION
294→% 
295→% Conclude the letter and express your gratitude to the editor and reviewers.
296→% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
297→\section*{Conclusion}
298→We are confident that the revisions have significantly enhanced the quality, clarity, and rigor of our manuscript. The constructive feedback from the reviewers has allowed us to refine our work and present a more accurate and comprehensive study. We believe that the revised manuscript now meets the high standards expected by the ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing, and we sincerely hope it will be considered favorably for publication.\\
299→
300→In the following attachment, we provide our responses to reviewer comments and suggestions in point-by-point track changes by color highlighting. The revision has been undertaken with the consent of all co-authors, and their contributions are duly acknowledged. Once again, we thank you and the reviewers for your invaluable input and the opportunity to improve our work. We look forward to your feedback on our revised submission.\\
301→
302→Sincerely,
303→
304→Getnet Demil on behalf of all co-authors
305→
306→Center for Machine Vision and Signal Analysis (CMVS), University of Oulu, Finland
307→
308→\clearpage
309→% The reviewer_comments.tex file should contain the original comments from the reviewers.
310→\input{reviewer_comments}
311→
312→\bibliographystyle{apalike}
313→\bibliography{biblography}
314→\end{document}
