\item \textbf{Dataset Scope.} Our test set covers 4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region through rigorous 70/15/15 train/val/test split with 30-day temporal separation. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. The test set's 18,117 patches from unseen tiles provide strong evidence for spatial transferability, but year-round operational readiness requires multi-season evaluation. Future work should assess performance in mid-latitude regions with sporadic snow cover, forested environments with canopy interference, maritime vs. continental snow regimes, and high-altitude vs. lowland environments.

\item \added{\textbf{Geographic Transferability.} Our study area---northern Finland's boreal zone---features relatively flat to gently undulating terrain (typical slopes $<$5\textdegree), homogeneous boreal forest land cover, and predominantly dry continental snow. These conditions are favorable for SAR-based fusion: gentle topography minimizes SAR geometric distortions, and dry snow exhibits relatively stable C-band backscatter characteristics \cite{ulaby1986microwave, nagler2000improved}. Consequently, our reported performance metrics may represent a near-optimal scenario for SAR-optical fusion architectures, and transferability to other geographic settings cannot be assumed without validation.

In complex alpine terrain (e.g., Alps, Himalayas, Andes), three interacting factors would challenge our approach. \textit{First}, SAR geometric distortions scale nonlinearly with terrain slope relative to the radar look angle. At Sentinel-1's nominal incidence angles (29\textdegree--46\textdegree), foreslopes exceeding approximately 20\textdegree\ produce severe foreshortening where topographic relief is compressed in the range direction, while slopes approaching the complement of the incidence angle cause layover where multiple ground elevations map to the same range cell \cite{tsai2019remote}. Radar shadow on steep back-slopes creates complete signal voids. These distortions are most severe in precisely the high-altitude regions where accurate snow monitoring is most critical for water resource management \cite{immerzeel2010large}. \textit{Second}, snow metamorphism varies substantially across climatic regimes: maritime snowpacks (e.g., Scandinavian coast, Cascades) are warm, dense, and undergo frequent melt--freeze cycles that dramatically alter C-band backscatter through wet-snow absorption \cite{nagler2016advancements}; continental interior snowpacks (e.g., Rocky Mountains, Siberia) remain cold and dry with depth hoar and faceted grain structures; and transitional regimes (e.g., Himalayan basins) exhibit complex stratigraphic layering with diurnal melt--refreeze at different elevations \cite{snapir2019spatiotemporal}. These regime-dependent SAR-optical relationships mean that feature representations learned in boreal Finland may not transfer without recalibration. \textit{Third}, the strong coupling between elevation, aspect, solar illumination geometry, and snow distribution in mountainous terrain creates spatial patterns fundamentally different from our study area, where snow distribution is primarily governed by vegetation canopy density and latitude rather than topographic position.

Several architectural properties of XFuse are designed to accommodate variable data quality, though their effectiveness in alpine terrain remains untested. The bidirectional cross-attention mechanism enables each modality to selectively attend to the other through learned query-key-value projections. In regions where SAR is degraded by geometric distortions or shadow, the attention weights could theoretically shift toward optical spectral features---analogous to the mechanism's designed behavior under cloud contamination, but operating in the reverse direction. The quality-aware training framework, which learns to weight samples by data reliability using heterogeneous metadata, could potentially be extended to incorporate terrain-derived quality indicators (e.g., local incidence angle, layover and shadow masks computed from DEM analysis) as additional metadata channels, enabling the model to systematically down-weight SAR observations in geometrically distorted regions.

However, we emphasize that these remain architectural affordances rather than demonstrated capabilities. Robust geographic generalization would require: (1) radiometric terrain correction of Sentinel-1 data using high-resolution DEMs to mitigate slope-dependent radiometric distortions; (2) incorporation of topographic variables (slope, aspect, curvature, sky view factor) as auxiliary model inputs, potentially through a dedicated terrain encoder path; (3) training on geographically diverse datasets spanning boreal, alpine, and maritime snow regimes to learn regime-aware SAR-optical relationships; and (4) domain adaptation or transfer learning techniques for efficiently extending learned representations to new snow climate zones with limited labeled data. We consider multi-region validation across contrasting terrain and snow climate zones as the primary direction for extending this work toward globally applicable operational deployment.}

\item \textbf{Comparison Scope.}

