\subsection{Limitations and Challenges}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dataset Scope:} Our test set covers 4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region through rigorous 70/15/15 train/val/test split with 30-day temporal separation. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. The test set's 18,117 patches from unseen tiles provide strong evidence for spatial transferability, but year-round operational readiness requires multi-season evaluation. Future work should assess performance in mid-latitude regions with sporadic snow cover, forested environments with canopy interference, maritime vs. continental snow regimes, and high-altitude vs. lowland environments.

    \item \textbf{Comparison Scope:} We compare against three strong baseline architectures (Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only) trained on identical data, demonstrating 38.7\% MAE reduction and 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement over the best baseline. We also reference published methods (HybridSN, CRformer, CVTNet, mSwinUNet) where metrics are available. However, direct comparison with operational snow products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F, Sentinel-3 SLSTR) and traditional NDSI thresholding remains future work. Our focus on architectural innovations and systematic ablation studies demonstrates methodological contributions, but operational validation requires benchmarking against existing operational systems under identical evaluation protocols.

    \item \textbf{Computational Requirements:} Our hybrid architecture contains 47.1M parameters and requires 4.2GB GPU memory during inference, processing 16$\times$16 patches in $\sim$50ms on A100 GPU. Training completed in 34.6 hours on a single NVIDIA A30 GPU, demonstrating feasibility for standard research infrastructure. However, continental or global-scale deployment at full temporal resolution (daily Sentinel-1/2 acquisitions) would require distributed computing infrastructure or model compression techniques (pruning, quantization, knowledge distillation) to achieve operational throughput. Test-time augmentation increases inference time by 8$\times$ but provides significant reliability improvements, creating trade-offs between accuracy and computational efficiency that must be balanced based on application requirements.

    \item \textbf{Reference Data Limitations:} Training relies on Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) at 60m resolution as reference data, inheriting GFSC's own accuracy constraints. \added{Consequently, all quantitative metrics reported in this study (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, $R^2$) are computed at 60m resolution and represent consistency with the GFSC product rather than independently validated accuracy. The model's 10m prediction capability, achieved through guided disaggregation during inference, is validated qualitatively by visual alignment with high-resolution Sentinel-2 optical boundaries but lacks quantitative ground truth at sub-60m scales.} While our multi-resolution framework successfully leverages 10m input features through the $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy, fine-scale snow cover heterogeneity below 60m lacks direct supervision. The validation approach comparing predictions against GFSC may overestimate performance relative to independent ground truth. Future work should incorporate sparse high-resolution annotations from field surveys, airborne LiDAR, or UAV imagery to validate sub-60m prediction accuracy and provide complementary ground truth independent of GFSC.

    \item \textbf{Quality Metadata Dependency:} The quality-aware framework integrates auxiliary metadata (Sentinel-2 SCL, cloud masks, GFSC QC layers, atmospheric parameters) through multiplicative loss weighting. While this enhances training robustness, operational deployment requires consistent availability of quality information across all satellite acquisitions. Some older archive data or degraded processing scenarios may lack complete metadata. Robust fallback strategies (learned quality assessment, default weighting schemes) are essential for scenarios where quality information is incomplete or unreliable.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Broader Implications for Earth Observation}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The methodological contributions of this work extend beyond snow cover monitoring to broader earth observation applications facing similar challenges. The multi-resolution training paradigm addresses a fundamental problem in remote sensing where high-resolution sensors lack corresponding high-resolution reference data. This approach is applicable to diverse applications including land cover classification, vegetation monitoring, and urban change detection, where training labels are often available only at coarser resolutions than input sensors.

