\textit{``The study focuses on 29 tiles in Northern Finland. [...] How does XFuse handle complex alpine terrain (Alps or Himalayas) where SAR geometric distortions are severe? The current dataset might not sufficiently challenge the cross-attention fusion mechanism's ability to handle missing SAR information due to shadows.''}
\end{quote}
We thank the reviewer for raising this critical question, which addresses perhaps the most significant limitation of our current evaluation. The reviewer is entirely correct: northern Finland's boreal landscape represents a favorable scenario for SAR-based fusion, and we agree that geographic transferability is the primary open question for our methodology. We have added a substantial new ``Geographic Transferability'' paragraph as Limitation \#2 in Section~5.5 that addresses each of the reviewer's specific concerns in detail.

\textbf{(1) SAR Geometric Distortions in Alpine Terrain:}

Northern Finland's gentle topography (typical slopes $<$5\textdegree) effectively minimizes the geometric distortions that plague SAR observations in mountainous regions. In alpine terrain, the interaction between Sentinel-1's nominal incidence angles (29\textdegree--46\textdegree) and steep slopes produces three distinct degradation mechanisms:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Foreshortening:} On foreslopes exceeding $\sim$20\textdegree, topographic relief is compressed in the range direction, degrading spatial resolution and mixing returns from different elevations.
    \item \textit{Layover:} When slope angle approaches the complement of the incidence angle, returns from multiple ground positions collapse into the same range cell, producing physically uninterpretable pixels.
    \item \textit{Radar shadow:} Steep back-slopes receive no radar illumination, creating complete signal voids.
\end{itemize}
These effects concentrate in precisely the high-altitude accumulation zones where snow monitoring is most critical for water resource management. Our results should therefore be interpreted as achievable under near-optimal SAR acquisition geometry.

\textbf{(2) Snow Metamorphism and Regime-Dependent SAR Signatures:}

The reviewer correctly identifies that SAR-snow interactions are highly dependent on snow type. Our study area's predominantly dry continental snow exhibits relatively stable C-band backscatter. In contrast:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Maritime snowpacks} (warm, dense, frequent melt--freeze) produce strong C-band absorption through liquid water content, causing dramatic backscatter changes during diurnal cycles.
    \item \textit{Continental interior snowpacks} (cold, dry, faceted grains) exhibit volume scattering from depth hoar layers.
    \item \textit{Transitional regimes} (e.g., Himalayan basins) display complex elevation-dependent diurnal stratification.
\end{itemize}
Feature representations learned from boreal Finland may not generalize to these different physical regimes without recalibration or domain adaptation.

\textbf{(3) How the Architecture Could Address These Challenges:}

While we have not validated XFuse in alpine terrain, two architectural design choices are directly relevant to the reviewer's concern about ``handling missing SAR information'':
\begin{itemize}
    \item The \textit{bidirectional cross-attention mechanism} enables each modality to selectively attend to the complementary modality through learned query-key-value projections. In SAR-degraded regions (shadow, layover), the cross-attention weights could theoretically shift toward optical spectral features---analogous to the mechanism's designed behavior under cloud contamination, but operating in the reverse modality direction. The asymmetric nature of cross-attention (each modality independently queries the other) is specifically designed for scenarios where one sensor provides degraded information.
    \item The \textit{quality-aware training framework} already learns continuous sample weighting from heterogeneous metadata sources (SCL, AOT, WVP, QC flags). This framework provides a natural extension point: terrain-derived quality indicators---local incidence angle, DEM-based layover/shadow masks---could be incorporated as additional metadata channels, enabling the model to systematically down-weight geometrically distorted SAR observations during training.
\end{itemize}
However, we are careful to present these as \textit{architectural affordances} rather than demonstrated capabilities. Without validation on mountainous datasets, we cannot claim that the cross-attention mechanism will successfully adapt to SAR shadow regions in practice.

\textbf{(4) Concrete Path Forward:}

We identify four requirements for robust geographic generalization in the revised manuscript:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Radiometric terrain correction of Sentinel-1 data using high-resolution DEMs
    \item Incorporation of topographic variables (slope, aspect, curvature, sky view factor) as auxiliary inputs
    \item Training on geographically diverse datasets spanning boreal, alpine, and maritime snow regimes
    \item Domain adaptation techniques for efficient transfer to undersampled snow climate zones
\end{enumerate}
We frame multi-region validation as the primary direction for future work and state explicitly that our current results represent performance under favorable SAR conditions.

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section~5.5 (new Limitation \#2: ``Geographic Transferability''---comprehensive discussion of SAR geometric distortions in alpine terrain, snow metamorphism regime effects, architectural mitigation properties, and concrete future work requirements).

\textbf{R2-3: Baseline comparison fairness (multi-resolution head)}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Are these baselines also equipped with the Multi-Resolution Processing (10m$\to$60m$\to$10m) head? If XFuse has the advantage of the guided upsampling head while baselines simply interpolate 60m predictions, the comparison is unfair.''}
\end{quote}
We appreciate this important question regarding experimental fairness. We confirm that \textbf{all baselines are trained and evaluated under identical conditions}, ensuring a fair comparison:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Identical data pipeline:} All models-Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only, and XFuse-use the same dataset class (\texttt{EnhancedDualResolutionDataset} with \\ \texttt{train\_mode=True}), which aggregates Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs from native 10m resolution to 60m via mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks. No model receives higher-resolution inputs than any other.
    \item \textbf{Identical training targets:} All models predict against the same 60m GFSC reference labels. All quantitative metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, F1, $R^2$) are computed at 60m resolution.
    \item \textbf{Multi-resolution head is inference-only:} The guided disaggregation ($60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) described in Section~3.2 is a post-processing step applied \textit{after} the core prediction during inference. It is \textbf{not} used during training or quantitative evaluation, and therefore does not contribute to any reported metric.
    \item \textbf{Ablation evidence confirms architectural gains:} The ablation study (Table~3) shows that removing multi-resolution processing reduces accuracy by only 1.76 percentage points (94.66\% $\rightarrow$ 92.90\%). Critically, XFuse \textit{without} multi-resolution processing (92.90\%) still outperforms the best baseline (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) by 3.77 percentage points, confirming that the performance gains are attributable to the cross-attention fusion and transformer components, not the resolution strategy.
\end{itemize}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Snow cover represents one of the most dynamic and influential components of the Earth's cryosphere, affecting an estimated 1.9 billion people who depend on snow-fed rivers for their water supply \cite{mankin2015potential}. Acting as both a massive freshwater reservoir and a critical climate regulator through its high albedo, snow cover exerts profound influence on regional and global climate systems, hydrological cycles, and socioeconomic systems \cite{siirila2021low, avanzi2024winter}. The seasonal snow cover extent has decreased significantly over recent decades, with Northern Hemisphere spring snow cover declining at rates of 1.6\% per decade for March-April and up to 11.7\% per decade for June since 1967 \cite{mudryk2020historical}, making accurate, timely snow monitoring essential for understanding and adapting to climate change impacts. Operational requirements for snow monitoring are demanding: high spatial resolution to capture fine-scale heterogeneity in mountainous terrain, frequent temporal coverage to track rapid snowmelt events, and robust all-weather capability to ensure continuity during storm events when snow accumulation is most dynamic.

Current approaches face fundamental limitations that constrain their operational utility. Optical sensors like MODIS, VIIRS, and Sentinel-2 provide rich spectral information through indices such as the Normalized Difference Snow Index (NDSI), exploiting snow's distinctive high visible reflectance and low shortwave infrared response \cite{stillinger2022landsat}. However, optical remote sensing faces a critical paradox: the meteorological conditions that produce snowfall, primarily cloud cover, are precisely those that render optical sensors ineffective. Persistent cloud cover in mountainous regions can obscure snow-covered areas for weeks, creating temporal gaps that severely limit operational applications. This systematic observational bias means the most dynamic periods of snow accumulation become invisible precisely when observation is most valuable. Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) systems offer all-weather imaging capability, penetrating clouds to detect wet snow through distinctive backscatter reduction caused by liquid water in the snowpack \cite{murfitt2024forward}. However, SAR-based snow detection faces its own challenges: the relationship between SAR backscatter and snow properties is inherently ambiguous, with changes in backscatter resulting from variations in snow grain size, liquid water content, layering structure, or underlying terrain characteristics \cite{hoppinen2024evaluating}. SAR's insensitivity to dry snow, whose dielectric properties mirror bare ground, creates complementary blind spots that single-modality approaches cannot resolve \cite{pan2023snow}. These complementary strengths and weaknesses naturally suggest multi-modal fusion as a pathway forward, yet existing fusion approaches face critical challenges that limit their effectiveness.

\subsection{Challenges in Multi-Modal Snow Cover Estimation}

Current fusion strategies predominantly employ either early fusion (concatenating raw sensor data) or late fusion (combining independent predictions), both of which fail to capture the complex, non-linear relationships between SAR backscatter patterns and optical spectral signatures \cite{yu2025stsnet}. Early fusion treats different modalities as simply additional channels, ignoring their fundamentally different physical measurement principles, while late fusion lacks mechanisms for cross-modal information exchange during feature learning. Beyond methodological limitations, operational satellite missions exhibit inherent multi-resolution characteristics that create fundamental training-inference gaps. While Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 provide data at 10--20m resolution, many reference snow cover products, including widely-used datasets like MODIS and the Copernicus Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) product, are available only at coarser resolutions ($\geq$60m) \cite{hoppinen2024evaluating}. Models trained on coarse-resolution targets may fail to leverage fine-scale information available in high-resolution input data, while direct downscaling approaches often introduce artifacts and fail to preserve physical consistency. Real-world satellite observations also exhibit highly variable quality characteristics due to atmospheric conditions, sensor calibration drift, geometric distortions, and processing artifacts \cite{poussin2025snow}. Traditional machine learning approaches treat all training samples equally, effectively assuming homogeneous data quality, an assumption systematically violated in operational satellite data streams. This quality heterogeneity can lead to degraded model performance and poor generalization when models encounter data quality conditions not well-represented in training sets. Furthermore, most existing approaches lack explicit uncertainty quantification, providing point predictions without reliability indicators essential for operational decision-making in critical applications such as flood forecasting and water resource planning.

Recent advances in deep learning, particularly attention mechanisms and vision transformer architectures, provide new opportunities for addressing these challenges \cite{hidalgo2024synergistic, ding2023multi}. The key insight driving our work is that effective multi-modal fusion requires explicit modeling of cross-modal relationships, understanding not just what each sensor observes independently, but how observations from different sensors relate to and inform each other. However, adapting these techniques to earth observation requires careful consideration of unique characteristics: high-dimensional data, spatial correlation structures, and physical constraints that differ fundamentally from natural language or computer vision domains where transformers were originally developed.

\subsection{Contributions}

This work introduces a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR (10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 bands unified at 10m) to generate high-resolution fractional snow cover maps with calibrated uncertainty estimates. Our approach addresses the identified challenges through three principal methodological innovations. First, we develop a novel multi-resolution processing framework that operates at 10m input resolution, aggregates features to 60m for supervised training against GFSC reference data, and produces enhanced 10m predictions through guided disaggregation, enabling training on widely-available coarse-resolution labels while exploiting fine-scale input information. Second, we design a cross-attention fusion mechanism that explicitly models bidirectional dependencies between SAR backscatter and optical spectral responses, capturing complex inter-sensor relationships missed by traditional concatenation or ensemble approaches. Third, we incorporate quality-aware training with multiplicative loss weighting based on multi-source metadata (GFSC QC layers, Sentinel-2 AOT/WVP/SCL), enabling robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data while providing well-calibrated uncertainty estimates. The hybrid architecture combines dual-path CNN backbones for modality-specific feature extraction, cross-attention fusion for intelligent multi-modal integration, and spatial transformers (6 layers, 8 heads, 512-dim) for global context modeling. Quality-aware training employs adaptive loss weighting ($\text{weight} = (\text{confidence}^\alpha) \times (\text{quality\_mask}^\beta)$) with curriculum learning that progressively relaxes quality thresholds during training. The uncertainty quantification framework provides spatially coherent confidence estimates that enable targeted quality control and risk-informed decision-making in downstream applications.

We provide comprehensive evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland with rigorous 70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test split and 30-day temporal separation, evaluating on 18,117 patches from 4 unseen geographic tiles. Our model achieves state-of-the-art performance: 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines trained on identical data. Compared to CNN-Only baseline (89.13\% accuracy, 22.46\% MAE), our hybrid architecture achieves 38.7\% MAE reduction (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Early Fusion (simple concatenation) achieves only 83.98\% accuracy with 25.20\% MAE, while Transformer-Only reaches 85.35\% accuracy with 27.05\% MAE, validating the necessity of hybrid CNN-Transformer design. Statistical validation confirms strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$; $R^2=0.588$ explaining 58.8\% variance) and physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$), substantially exceeding baselines: CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). The 24.9 percentage point improvement in explained variance over CNN-Only ($R^2$: 0.239 $\rightarrow$ 0.588) represents 2.5$\times$ better performance. Systematic ablation studies validate each component: removing cross-attention causes 5.16 point accuracy drop (largest individual contribution), removing transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points, demonstrating synergistic benefits of the hybrid design. Critically, uncertainty quantification achieves excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025, well below the 0.03 threshold, with mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching observed error rate and spatially coherent patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$) with elevated values at snow-land boundaries. Test-time augmentation enhances accuracy by 2.3\% with 22.4\% reduction in uncertainty variance. While comprehensive comparative evaluation against operational products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F), multi-regional assessment across diverse climatic conditions, and multi-season temporal validation remain essential future work for operational deployment, the current evaluation demonstrates architectural superiority over strong baselines and excellent calibration properties. The methodological contributions include multi-resolution training, cross-modal attention fusion, and quality-aware learning, which have broad implications for earth observation applications beyond snow monitoring, addressing fundamental challenges in learning from heterogeneous satellite data with resolution mismatches and variable quality characteristics.

\subsection{Paper Organization}

The remainder of this paper is organised as follows. Section \ref{sec:related_work} reviews related work in multi-modal remote sensing, deep learning architectures for earth observation, and quality-aware machine learning. Section \ref{sec:proposed_method} presents our methodology, including detailed architectural descriptions and training procedures. Section \ref{sec:result} describes experimental setup, datasets, and evaluation protocols and presents comprehensive results including quantitative performance analysis and uncertainty quantification. Section \ref{sec:discussion}  discusses implications, limitations, and future research directions. Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes with a summary of contributions and broader impacts.

