    \label{fig:uncertainty_analysis}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Spatial Uncertainty Patterns}

Spatial analysis of uncertainty maps reveals physically meaningful patterns with elevated uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries, areas with complex topographic features, and regions affected by atmospheric interference. The uncertainty framework provides interpretable confidence estimates for downstream applications, with uncertainty varying appropriately based on data quality and surface characteristics. Spatial autocorrelation analysis using Moran's I statistic yields a value of $0.548$ ($p = 0.002$), indicating moderate positive spatial autocorrelation. This confirms physically realistic spatial patterns where neighboring pixels exhibit correlated predictions, consistent with the continuous nature of snow cover distribution. The statistically significant autocorrelation validates that the model captures genuine spatial structure rather than producing spatially random predictions.

\subsubsection{Uncertainty-Error Correlation}

Analysis of the relationship between predicted uncertainty and prediction errors reveals strong negative correlation ($r = -0.563, p < 0.001$), confirming that higher uncertainty appropriately corresponds to larger prediction errors. This inverse relationship validates the utility of uncertainty estimates for identifying unreliable predictions in operational scenarios, enabling quality-controlled snow cover mapping where low-confidence predictions can be flagged for additional validation.

\subsection{Qualitative Results}

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images//results/Prediction_main_result_s02.png}
    \caption{Representative qualitative results showing Sentinel-1 SAR dual-polarization backscatter (VV and VH) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery as model inputs (top row). Model outputs (bottom row) show fractional snow cover predictions, binary classification results, and spatially coherent uncertainty maps. The model successfully captures fine-scale spatial patterns with sharp boundaries at snow-land transitions. Uncertainty estimates appropriately increase at transition zones while maintaining low values in homogeneous regions, demonstrating excellent model calibration with uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries and complex terrain.}
    \label{fig:qualitative}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative} presents representative test samples demonstrating our model's superior spatial detail preservation and uncertainty quantification. Compared to baselines (not shown), our hybrid architecture produces smoother, more realistic snow cover maps with accurate boundary delineation. Early Fusion exhibits noisy predictions with false positives in non-snow regions due to its inability to capture complex inter-modal relationships. CNN-Only achieves better smoothness but misses fine-scale snow patterns in complex terrain due to lack of global context. Transformer-Only produces spatially inconsistent results, lacking the local feature extraction necessary for precise boundary localization. Our model successfully balances spatial smoothness with detail preservation, accurately capturing gradual snow cover transitions and complex topographic effects. The spatially coherent uncertainty maps provide operationally valuable confidence information, with elevated uncertainty at ambiguous boundaries and low uncertainty in homogeneous regions. The model generalizes well to diverse test scenarios including forest edges, water bodies, and urban areas.

\subsection{Robustness to Cloud Contamination}
\label{sec:cloud_robustness}

\added{Cloud contamination is the central motivation for SAR-optical fusion: optical sensors like Sentinel-2 cannot penetrate clouds, while Sentinel-1 SAR provides all-weather imaging capability. We address this through two complementary mechanisms and provide direct experimental evidence of SAR's compensating role.

\textbf{Architectural robustness via quality-aware training.} Our quality-aware training framework (Section~\ref{sec:quality_aware}) explicitly incorporates Sentinel-2 Scene Classification Layer (SCL) cloud masks as per-pixel loss weights. Cloud-contaminated pixels (SCL classes 7--9: low/medium/high probability cloud; class 10: thin cirrus; class 3: cloud shadow) receive downweighted loss contributions, ensuring the model learns robust SAR-optical relationships rather than memorising cloud artefacts. This design was deliberate: the model is trained to leverage SAR structure when optical signals are unreliable.

\textbf{Simulated cloud occlusion experiment.} Quantifying cloud robustness is constrained by the operational reality that cloud-free ground truth is required for quantitative evaluation---the same clear-sky condition that renders optical sensors functional. Our test acquisitions (July 2025, northern Finland) present cloud-free conditions precisely because these are the scenarios where paired optical reference data is obtainable. To evaluate SAR's compensating role under cloud conditions, we conduct a \textit{simulated cloud occlusion experiment}: we run inference on all 18,117 test patches with all Sentinel-2 input channels zeroed (simulating 100\% optical loss), leaving only Sentinel-1 SAR as the information source.

Table~\ref{tab:cloud_robustness} presents results. XFuse with complete S2 occlusion achieves \textbf{83.2\%} accuracy and 17.6\% MAE, representing only an 8.7 percentage point degradation from full S1+S2 performance. Critically, \textbf{XFuse with no optical input (83.2\%) outperforms Early Fusion with its full S1+S2 input (81.3\%)}. This demonstrates that the cross-attention fusion mechanism learns to leverage SAR features independently of optical data: when S2 is unavailable, the bidirectional attention re-weights to draw information from S1, maintaining meaningful predictive capability. Optical baselines have no analogous fallback: Early Fusion concatenation and CNN-Only treat both modalities symmetrically and cannot compensate for missing optical channels.}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\added{
\caption{Simulated cloud occlusion experiment: Sentinel-2 channels set to zero (SAR-only inference) vs. full S1+S2 input. XFuse without optical data outperforms Early Fusion with full input.}
\label{tab:cloud_robustness}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcc|cc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Input} & \textbf{Acc.~(\%)} & \textbf{MAE~(\%)} & \textbf{$\Delta$ Acc.} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{XFuse (Ours)} & S1 + S2 (full) & 91.9 & 13.8 & --- \\
                               & S1 only (S2$=\mathbf{0}$) & \textbf{83.2} & \textbf{17.6} & $-$8.7 pp \\
\midrule
Early Fusion & S1 + S2 (full) & 81.3 & 25.3 & --- \\
CNN-Only     & S1 + S2 (full) & 87.5 & 22.5 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize S2$=\mathbf{0}$: all Sentinel-2 channels zeroed; model relies purely on Sentinel-1 SAR.}
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\subsection{Test-Time Augmentation and Ensemble Robustness}

\subsubsection{TTA Performance Enhancement}

Test-time augmentation (TTA) implementation significantly enhances prediction reliability across all test samples. Eight-fold geometric augmentation (rotation, reflection) improves accuracy by 2.3\% on average while reducing uncertainty by 22.4\%. This ensemble approach provides robust prediction intervals with accuracy gains ranging from 1.5\% to 3.0\% across different samples, confirming the value of TTA for operational snow monitoring applications.

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{images//results/tta_analysis_s00.png}
    \caption{Test-time augmentation analysis. Eight-fold geometric augmentation (rotation, reflection) improves accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%, demonstrating enhanced prediction reliability through ensemble averaging.}
    \label{fig:tta_analysis}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Ensemble Variance Reduction}

Ensemble variance analysis demonstrates successful uncertainty reduction through augmentation averaging. The 22.4\% reduction in uncertainty variance indicates that TTA effectively captures prediction variability arising from orientation-dependent features, while maintaining prediction accuracy. This enhancement validates the importance of rotational and reflectional invariance in snow cover mapping applications.

\subsection{Computational Efficiency}

\textbf{Model Complexity.} Our hybrid architecture contains 47.1M parameters, compared to 12.8M (Early Fusion), 26.2M (CNN-Only), and 21.3M (Transformer-Only). Despite increased complexity, the performance gains justify the additional parameters: 38.7\% MAE reduction over CNN-Only represents substantial practical improvement for operational snow monitoring. Training completed in 34.6 hours on a single NVIDIA A30 GPU with batch size 8, demonstrating feasibility for standard research infrastructure.

\textbf{Inference Speed.} The model processes 16$\times$16 patches (960m$\times$960m ground coverage at 60m resolution) with $\sim$50ms inference time per patch on A100 GPU. For the entire test set (18,117 patches), inference requires $\sim$15 minutes. Batch processing with batch size 32 achieves throughput of $\sim$2,000 patches/minute, enabling rapid large-scale deployment. Peak GPU memory consumption is 4.2GB during inference, allowing deployment on consumer-grade hardware.

\textbf{Scalability.} For full satellite tile processing, sliding window extraction with configurable stride enables trade-off between spatial coverage and computational cost. The model's multi-resolution design naturally handles varying input sizes, supporting operational deployment at continental scales. Test-time augmentation increases inference time by 8$\times$ but provides significant accuracy and uncertainty improvements, making it suitable for high-stakes operational applications where prediction reliability is critical.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}

\textbf{Dataset Scope.} Our test set covers 4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. Future work should evaluate on multi-season, multi-region datasets to assess year-round operational readiness and transferability to different snow conditions (maritime vs. continental snow, high-altitude vs. lowland environments).

\textbf{Comparison Scope.} We compare against three baseline architectures trained on identical data and reference published methods where metrics are available. Comprehensive comparison with operational snow products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F, Sentinel-3 SLSTR) and additional fusion architectures remains future work. Our focus on architectural innovations and systematic ablation studies aligns with demonstrating methodological contributions, but operational validation requires direct comparison with existing operational systems.

\textbf{Application Domain.} While we demonstrate effectiveness for fractional snow cover mapping, our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture with cross-modal attention, multi-resolution processing, and quality-aware learning should transfer to other remote sensing tasks (land cover classification, change detection, crop monitoring, disaster assessment). Evaluating task transferability and domain adaptation represents promising future research directions.

