\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Snow cover represents one of the most dynamic and influential components of the Earth's cryosphere, affecting an estimated 1.9 billion people who depend on snow-fed rivers for their water supply \cite{mankin2015potential}. Acting as both a massive freshwater reservoir and a critical climate regulator through its high albedo, snow cover exerts profound influence on regional and global climate systems, hydrological cycles, and socioeconomic systems \cite{siirila2021low, avanzi2024winter}. The seasonal snow cover extent has decreased significantly over recent decades, with Northern Hemisphere spring snow cover declining at rates of 1.6\% per decade for March-April and up to 11.7\% per decade for June since 1967 \cite{mudryk2020historical}, making accurate, timely snow monitoring essential for understanding and adapting to climate change impacts. Operational requirements for snow monitoring are demanding: high spatial resolution to capture fine-scale heterogeneity in mountainous terrain, frequent temporal coverage to track rapid snowmelt events, and robust all-weather capability to ensure continuity during storm events when snow accumulation is most dynamic.

Current approaches face fundamental limitations that constrain their operational utility. Optical sensors like MODIS, VIIRS, and Sentinel-2 provide rich spectral information through indices such as the Normalized Difference Snow Index (NDSI), exploiting snow's distinctive high visible reflectance and low shortwave infrared response \cite{stillinger2022landsat}. However, optical remote sensing faces a critical paradox: the meteorological conditions that produce snowfall, primarily cloud cover, are precisely those that render optical sensors ineffective. Persistent cloud cover in mountainous regions can obscure snow-covered areas for weeks, creating temporal gaps that severely limit operational applications. This systematic observational bias means the most dynamic periods of snow accumulation become invisible precisely when observation is most valuable. Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) systems offer all-weather imaging capability, penetrating clouds to detect wet snow through distinctive backscatter reduction caused by liquid water in the snowpack \cite{murfitt2024forward}. However, SAR-based snow detection faces its own challenges: the relationship between SAR backscatter and snow properties is inherently ambiguous, with changes in backscatter resulting from variations in snow grain size, liquid water content, layering structure, or underlying terrain characteristics \cite{hoppinen2024evaluating}. SAR's insensitivity to dry snow, whose dielectric properties mirror bare ground, creates complementary blind spots that single-modality approaches cannot resolve \cite{pan2023snow}. These complementary strengths and weaknesses naturally suggest multi-modal fusion as a pathway forward, yet existing fusion approaches face critical challenges that limit their effectiveness.

\subsection{Challenges in Multi-Modal Snow Cover Estimation}

Current fusion strategies predominantly employ either early fusion (concatenating raw sensor data) or late fusion (combining independent predictions), both of which fail to capture the complex, non-linear relationships between SAR backscatter patterns and optical spectral signatures \cite{yu2025stsnet}. Early fusion treats different modalities as simply additional channels, ignoring their fundamentally different physical measurement principles, while late fusion lacks mechanisms for cross-modal information exchange during feature learning. Beyond methodological limitations, operational satellite missions exhibit inherent multi-resolution characteristics that create fundamental training-inference gaps. While Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 provide data at 10--20m resolution, many reference snow cover products, including widely-used datasets like MODIS and the Copernicus Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) product, are available only at coarser resolutions ($\geq$60m) \cite{hoppinen2024evaluating}. Models trained on coarse-resolution targets may fail to leverage fine-scale information available in high-resolution input data, while direct downscaling approaches often introduce artifacts and fail to preserve physical consistency. Real-world satellite observations also exhibit highly variable quality characteristics due to atmospheric conditions, sensor calibration drift, geometric distortions, and processing artifacts \cite{poussin2025snow}. Traditional machine learning approaches treat all training samples equally, effectively assuming homogeneous data quality, an assumption systematically violated in operational satellite data streams. This quality heterogeneity can lead to degraded model performance and poor generalization when models encounter data quality conditions not well-represented in training sets. Furthermore, most existing approaches lack explicit uncertainty quantification, providing point predictions without reliability indicators essential for operational decision-making in critical applications such as flood forecasting and water resource planning.

Recent advances in deep learning, particularly attention mechanisms and vision transformer architectures, provide new opportunities for addressing these challenges \cite{hidalgo2024synergistic, ding2023multi}. The key insight driving our work is that effective multi-modal fusion requires explicit modeling of cross-modal relationships, understanding not just what each sensor observes independently, but how observations from different sensors relate to and inform each other. However, adapting these techniques to earth observation requires careful consideration of unique characteristics: high-dimensional data, spatial correlation structures, and physical constraints that differ fundamentally from natural language or computer vision domains where transformers were originally developed.

\subsection{Contributions}

\deleted{This work introduces a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR (10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 bands unified at 10m) to generate high-resolution fractional snow cover maps with calibrated uncertainty estimates. Our approach addresses the identified challenges through three principal methodological innovations. First, we develop a novel multi-resolution processing framework that operates at 10m input resolution, aggregates features to 60m for supervised training against GFSC reference data, and produces enhanced 10m predictions through guided disaggregation, enabling training on widely-available coarse-resolution labels while exploiting fine-scale input information. Second, we design a cross-attention fusion mechanism that explicitly models bidirectional dependencies between SAR backscatter and optical spectral responses, capturing complex inter-sensor relationships missed by traditional concatenation or ensemble approaches. Third, we incorporate quality-aware training with multiplicative loss weighting based on multi-source metadata (GFSC QC layers, Sentinel-2 AOT/WVP/SCL), enabling robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data while providing well-calibrated uncertainty estimates. The hybrid architecture combines dual-path CNN backbones for modality-specific feature extraction, cross-attention fusion for intelligent multi-modal integration, and spatial transformers (6 layers, 8 heads, 512-dim) for global context modeling. Quality-aware training employs adaptive loss weighting ($\text{weight} = (\text{confidence}^\alpha) \times (\text{quality\_mask}^\beta)$) with curriculum learning that progressively relaxes quality thresholds during training. The uncertainty quantification framework provides spatially coherent confidence estimates that enable targeted quality control and risk-informed decision-making in downstream applications.}

\deleted{We provide comprehensive evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland with rigorous 70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test split and 30-day temporal separation, evaluating on 18,117 patches from 4 unseen geographic tiles. Our model achieves state-of-the-art performance: 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines trained on identical data. Compared to CNN-Only baseline (89.13\% accuracy, 22.46\% MAE), our hybrid architecture achieves 38.7\% MAE reduction (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Early Fusion (simple concatenation) achieves only 83.98\% accuracy with 25.20\% MAE, while Transformer-Only reaches 85.35\% accuracy with 27.05\% MAE, validating the necessity of hybrid CNN-Transformer design. Statistical validation confirms strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$; $R^2=0.588$ explaining 58.8\% variance) and physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$), substantially exceeding baselines: CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). The 24.9 percentage point improvement in explained variance over CNN-Only ($R^2$: 0.239 $\rightarrow$ 0.588) represents 2.5$\times$ better performance. Systematic ablation studies validate each component: removing cross-attention causes 5.16 point accuracy drop (largest individual contribution), removing transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points, demonstrating synergistic benefits of the hybrid design. Critically, uncertainty quantification achieves excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025, well below the 0.03 threshold, with mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching observed error rate and spatially coherent patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$) with elevated values at snow-land boundaries. Test-time augmentation enhances accuracy by 2.3\% with 22.4\% reduction in uncertainty variance. While comprehensive comparative evaluation against operational products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F), multi-regional assessment across diverse climatic conditions, and multi-season temporal validation remain essential future work for operational deployment, the current evaluation demonstrates architectural superiority over strong baselines and excellent calibration properties. The methodological contributions include multi-resolution training, cross-modal attention fusion, and quality-aware learning, which have broad implications for earth observation applications beyond snow monitoring, addressing fundamental challenges in learning from heterogeneous satellite data with resolution mismatches and variable quality characteristics.}

\added{This work introduces XFuse, a hybrid CNN--Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical imagery to generate fractional snow cover maps with calibrated uncertainty estimates.} \\
\added{The proposed approach addresses the identified challenges through three principal contributions:} \\
\added{1. \textbf{Bidirectional cross-attention fusion} that models asymmetric SAR--optical interdependencies through learned query--key--value projections, enabling each modality to selectively attend to complementary information from the other. Unlike concatenation or ensemble strategies that treat modalities symmetrically or independently, cross-attention captures how SAR structural information informs optical interpretation under cloud contamination, and how optical spectral signatures disambiguate SAR backscatter variations.} \\
\added{2. \textbf{Multi-resolution training strategy} ($10\,\mathrm{m} \rightarrow 60\,\mathrm{m} \rightarrow 10\,\mathrm{m}$) that fuses Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 features at native resolution, adaptively aggregates them to 60\,m for supervised training against GFSC reference labels, and subsequently disaggregates predictions through guided upsampling for spatial enhancement. This enables training with widely available coarse-resolution reference data while preserving fine-scale spatial information from high-resolution inputs.} \\
\added{3. \textbf{Quality-aware curriculum learning} that integrates heterogeneous metadata from multiple independent sources (Sentinel-2 cloud masks and atmospheric parameters, together with GFSC quality flags) via multiplicative loss weighting with progressive threshold scheduling. This strategy enables robust learning from variable-quality satellite observations while producing well-calibrated uncertainty estimates suitable for operational decision support.} \\
\added{Evaluation on Sentinel-1/2 data from northern Finland using geographically separated test tiles demonstrates state-of-the-art performance, achieving a 38.7\% reduction in MAE compared to the strongest baseline. Systematic ablation studies show that cross-attention fusion provides the largest individual performance gain, validating the proposed architectural design. Full experimental results, statistical validation, and uncertainty analysis are presented in Section~\ref{sec:result}.}


\subsection{Paper Organization}

The remainder of this paper is organised as follows. Section \ref{sec:related_work} reviews related work in multi-modal remote sensing, deep learning architectures for earth observation, and quality-aware machine learning. Section \ref{sec:proposed_method} presents our methodology, including detailed architectural descriptions and training procedures. Section \ref{sec:result} describes experimental setup, datasets, and evaluation protocols and presents comprehensive results including quantitative performance analysis and uncertainty quantification. Section \ref{sec:discussion}  discusses implications, limitations, and future research directions. Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes with a summary of contributions and broader impacts.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This work addressed the critical challenge of accurate, all-weather fractional snow cover estimation through a novel hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m). We introduced three principal methodological contributions that establish new paradigms for earth observation: (1) a multi-resolution processing framework implementing a novel $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy that enables learning from coarse-resolution labels (GFSC at 60m) while producing enhanced high-resolution predictions through learned aggregation and guided disaggregation, (2) a cross-modal attention fusion mechanism employing bidirectional query-key-value transformations to explicitly model inter-sensor dependencies between SAR backscatter and optical spectral responses, and (3) a quality-aware training paradigm that leverages auxiliary metadata from multiple sources (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) through multiplicative loss weighting and curriculum learning. The integrated architecture combines dual-path CNN backbones with modality-specific attention (CBAM for SAR, ECA for optical), cross-attention fusion for intelligent multi-modal integration, and spatial transformers (6 layers, 8 heads, 512-dim) for global context modeling.

Comprehensive experimental evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland with rigorous 70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test split and 30-day temporal separation demonstrated state-of-the-art performance on 18,117 test patches from 4 geographically unseen tiles. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy with 78.71\% F1-score, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Statistical validation confirmed strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$) with coefficient of determination $R^2=0.588$ (explaining 58.8\% variance), substantially exceeding baselines: CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). Spatial autocorrelation analysis (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$) validated physically realistic spatial patterns. Systematic ablation studies confirmed each component's contribution: removing cross-attention caused 5.16 point accuracy drop (largest individual contribution), removing transformer reduced accuracy by 3.86 points, demonstrating synergistic benefits of the hybrid design. Critically, uncertainty quantification achieved excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025 (well below the 0.03 threshold), mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching observed error rate, and spatially coherent patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$) with elevated values at snow-land boundaries. Test-time augmentation enhanced accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%. Computational efficiency analysis confirmed operational feasibility: 47.1M parameters, 4.2GB GPU memory during inference, $\sim$50ms per patch on A100 GPU, enabling throughput of $\sim$2,000 patches/minute with batch processing.

The methodological advances introduced in this work establish new paradigms for earth observation applications. The multi-resolution training strategy addresses the fundamental challenge of leveraging high-resolution satellite sensors when training labels exist only at coarser resolutions, with broad applicability to land cover classification, vegetation monitoring, and urban change detection. The quality-aware learning framework provides systematic methods for learning from heterogeneous satellite archives while quantifying uncertainty, enabling responsible deployment of machine learning in operational contexts where reliability is paramount. The cross-modal attention fusion mechanism demonstrates that explicit modeling of inter-sensor dependencies significantly outperforms simpler concatenation or ensemble approaches, providing a template for future multi-sensor earth observation systems. The demonstrated calibration of uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) with spatially coherent patterns enables targeted improvement strategies and supports probabilistic decision-making frameworks that propagate observation uncertainty through downstream applications.

From a practical deployment perspective, this work advances operational readiness for snow monitoring systems across multiple dimensions. The processing efficiency should be considered before processing regional satellite archives since it requires specialised computational infrastructure beyond standard GPU hardware. The all-weather capability, combining SAR's cloud-penetrating observations with optical imagery's superior surface discrimination, ensures continuous monitoring independent of atmospheric conditions. The well-calibrated uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) provide operational systems with trustworthy quantitative confidence bounds for risk-informed decision-making in applications ranging from flood forecasting to avalanche risk assessment. The demonstrated robustness across diverse snow coverage scenarios (0.2\%-75.5\%) and ability to handle varying data quality conditions confirm reliability under real-world operational conditions. These capabilities address critical operational requirements for water resource management, where accurate snow cover information supports reservoir operation, irrigation planning, and hydropower generation decisions affecting billions of people dependent on snow-fed water supplies.

Future research directions include:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Multi-Season and Multi-Region Validation.} Expanding evaluation beyond northern Finland winter 2025 to encompass diverse geographic regions (mid-latitudes, forested environments, arid zones), seasonal conditions (snowmelt periods, fall snow onset, spring transitions), and multi-year temporal coverage to comprehensively validate generalization capabilities and establish year-round operational readiness;

    \item \textbf{Operational Product Comparison.} Conducting systematic comparison with operational snow products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F, Sentinel-3 SLSTR, IMS) and traditional NDSI thresholding under identical evaluation protocols to quantify advantages relative to established baselines and contextualize performance within the operational snow monitoring landscape;

    \item \textbf{Model Compression and Efficiency.} Developing model compression techniques (quantization, pruning, knowledge distillation) to reduce the 47.1M parameter footprint and 4.2GB memory requirements, enabling deployment on resource-constrained computing environments and supporting continental-scale processing at full Sentinel-1/2 temporal resolution;

    \item \textbf{Task Transferability.} Evaluating the hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture with cross-modal attention fusion on related remote sensing applications (land cover classification, change detection, crop monitoring, disaster assessment) to validate transferability and demonstrate the methodological contributions' broader applicability beyond snow cover mapping;

    \item \textbf{Additional Modalities.} Incorporating additional data sources including SAR interferometric coherence for wet snow detection, multi-temporal NDSI time series for phenological context, L-band SAR for improved dry snow discrimination, and elevation-derived terrain parameters (slope, aspect, terrain ruggedness) to further enhance prediction accuracy in complex mountainous environments; and

    \item \textbf{Hydrological Integration.} Integrating fractional snow cover predictions with process-based snowmelt models and hydrological forecasting systems to evaluate end-to-end performance for operational water resource applications and validate downstream utility for reservoir operation, irrigation planning, and flood forecasting.
\end{enumerate}

The demonstrated success of our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture with cross-modal attention fusion, multi-resolution processing, and quality-aware learning establishes a foundation for next-generation earth observation systems. The substantial performance improvements over strong baselines (38.7\% MAE reduction, 5.5pp accuracy gain) combined with excellent uncertainty calibration (ECE=0.025) validate that explicit modeling of inter-sensor dependencies through bidirectional attention mechanisms, synergistic combination of CNNs for local feature extraction with Transformers for global context, and systematic integration of quality metadata enable robust operational deployment. These methodological advances provide a template for multi-sensor fusion systems that combine heterogeneous satellite data, handle variable quality conditions, and provide quantified uncertainties essential for responsible decision-making in critical earth observation applications during an era of accelerating environmental change.

