\section{Experiments and Results}
\label{sec:result}

\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2pt}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\textbf{Dataset and Data Splits.} We train and evaluate our hybrid CNN-Transformer fusion architecture on multi-modal remote sensing data combining Sentinel-1 GRDH SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m resolution) and Sentinel-2B/C optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m from native 10m/20m/60m resolutions) \cite{CopernicusDashboard}. Ground truth labels use Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) at 60m resolution \cite{CLMS_SnowCover_2016present}. The dataset comprises 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tile acquisitions from northern Finland spanning diverse conditions: snow coverage 0--100\%, varying cloud coverage, and heterogeneous land cover (forest, water, urban, bare ground). We employ a geographic split to ensure spatial independence: 70\% training (12 tiles, $\sim$54,000 patches), 15\% validation (3 tiles, $\sim$9,000 patches), and 15\% test (4 tiles, 18,117 patches). The 30-day temporal separation between splits prevents data leakage. Data preprocessing includes SAR radiometric calibration to sigma-naught backscatter, Enhanced Lee Sigma speckle filtering (7$\times$7 window), Range-Doppler terrain correction, and atmospheric correction for optical bands using hybrid Dark Object Subtraction and MODTRAN-based approach incorporating Rayleigh scattering, aerosol transmission (AOT at 550nm), and water vapor absorption corrections.

