\begin{abstract}
\deleted{Accurate fractional snow cover (FSC) estimation is critical for climate monitoring, hydrological modeling, and water resource management, yet current approaches face fundamental limitations. Optical sensors provide rich spectral information but are severely limited by cloud contamination, while Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) offers all-weather capability but lacks spectral richness for precise snow characterization. Existing multi-sensor fusion approaches either employ simplistic strategies like concatenation or ensemble that fail to capture complex inter-modal relationships, or ignore the inherent multi-resolution nature of operational satellite missions, limiting their ability to generate high-resolution predictions from coarse-resolution training labels. This paper introduces a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m from native 10m/20m/60m resolutions) to generate high-resolution fractional snow cover maps at 10m spatial resolution. The architecture addresses multi-resolution challenges through a novel $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy: S1/S2 features are fused at 10m through bidirectional cross-attention, aggregated to 60m for supervised training against Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) reference data, and disaggregated back to 10m through guided upsampling for enhanced spatial detail. Dual-path CNN backbones with modality-specific attention (CBAM for SAR, ECA for optical) extract hierarchical features, cross-attention fusion captures inter-sensor dependencies through explicit query-key-value transformations, and spatial transformers (6 layers, 8 heads, 512-dim) model global context. Quality-aware training incorporates multiplicative loss weighting based on multi-source metadata (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) with curriculum learning, enabling robust training from heterogeneous satellite data while providing calibrated uncertainty estimates. Comprehensive evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland (70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test with 30-day temporal separation, 18,117 test patches from 4 unseen tiles) demonstrates state-of-the-art performance. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Statistical validation confirms strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$; $R^2=0.588$ explaining 58.8\% variance) and physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$), substantially exceeding baselines (CNN-Only: $R^2=0.239$; Early Fusion: $R^2=0.026$). Systematic ablation studies validate each component's contribution: removing cross-attention causes 5.16 point accuracy drop, removing transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points. Critically, uncertainty quantification demonstrates excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025 $<$ 0.03 threshold, mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching the observed error rate, and spatially coherent patterns with elevated values at snow-land boundaries (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$). Test-time augmentation improves accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%. The methodological contributions in multi-resolution training, cross-modal attention fusion, quality-aware learning, have broad implications for earth observation applications requiring fusion of heterogeneous multi-resolution satellite data with quantified uncertainty.}

\added{Accurate fractional snow cover (FSC) mapping requires combining the complementary strengths of optical imagery (spectral discrimination) and Synthetic Aperture Radar (all-weather capability), yet existing fusion approaches employ simplistic concatenation strategies that fail to model complex inter-modal relationships, while ignoring the resolution mismatch between high-resolution inputs and coarse reference labels. We introduce XFuse, a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture that addresses these challenges through three key contributions: (1) \textbf{bidirectional cross-attention fusion} that explicitly models SAR-optical interdependencies via query-key-value transformations, enabling each modality to selectively attend to complementary information from the other; (2) a \textbf{multi-resolution training strategy} ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) that fuses Sentinel-1/2 features at native resolution, aggregates to 60m for supervision against Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) labels, and disaggregates through guided upsampling for enhanced spatial detail; and (3) \textbf{quality-aware curriculum learning} that incorporates multi-source metadata (cloud masks, atmospheric parameters, QC flags) through adaptive loss weighting, enabling robust training from heterogeneous satellite data while providing calibrated uncertainty estimates. Evaluation on 29 Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland (18,117 test patches from 4 geographically unseen tiles) demonstrates state-of-the-art performance: 13.77\% MAE and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing 38.7\% MAE reduction over the best baseline. Ablation studies confirm cross-attention as the largest individual contributor (5.16 percentage point accuracy gain), while uncertainty quantification achieves excellent calibration (ECE=0.025). The methodology generalizes to earth observation applications requiring fusion of heterogeneous multi-resolution satellite data.}

    \textbf{Keywords:} Fractional snow cover, multi-modal fusion, hybrid CNN-Transformer, cross-attention, multi-resolution processing, quality-aware learning, uncertainty quantification, Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2
    \end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Snow cover represents one of the most dynamic and influential components of the Earth's cryosphere, affecting an estimated 1.9 billion people who depend on snow-fed rivers for their water supply \cite{mankin2015potential}. Acting as both a massive freshwater reservoir and a critical climate regulator through its high albedo, snow cover exerts profound influence on regional and global climate systems, hydrological cycles, and socioeconomic systems \cite{siirila2021low, avanzi2024winter}. The seasonal snow cover extent has decreased significantly over recent decades, with Northern Hemisphere spring snow cover declining at rates of 1.6\% per decade for March-April and up to 11.7\% per decade for June since 1967 \cite{mudryk2020historical}, making accurate, timely snow monitoring essential for understanding and adapting to climate change impacts. Operational requirements for snow monitoring are demanding: high spatial resolution to capture fine-scale heterogeneity in mountainous terrain, frequent temporal coverage to track rapid snowmelt events, and robust all-weather capability to ensure continuity during storm events when snow accumulation is most dynamic.

Current approaches face fundamental limitations that constrain their operational utility. Optical sensors like MODIS, VIIRS, and Sentinel-2 provide rich spectral information through indices such as the Normalized Difference Snow Index (NDSI), exploiting snow's distinctive high visible reflectance and low shortwave infrared response \cite{stillinger2022landsat}. However, optical remote sensing faces a critical paradox: the meteorological conditions that produce snowfall, primarily cloud cover, are precisely those that render optical sensors ineffective. Persistent cloud cover in mountainous regions can obscure snow-covered areas for weeks, creating temporal gaps that severely limit operational applications. This systematic observational bias means the most dynamic periods of snow accumulation become invisible precisely when observation is most valuable. Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) systems offer all-weather imaging capability, penetrating clouds to detect wet snow through distinctive backscatter reduction caused by liquid water in the snowpack \cite{murfitt2024forward}. However, SAR-based snow detection faces its own challenges: the relationship between SAR backscatter and snow properties is inherently ambiguous, with changes in backscatter resulting from variations in snow grain size, liquid water content, layering structure, or underlying terrain characteristics \cite{hoppinen2024evaluating}. SAR's insensitivity to dry snow, whose dielectric properties mirror bare ground, creates complementary blind spots that single-modality approaches cannot resolve \cite{pan2023snow}. These complementary strengths and weaknesses naturally suggest multi-modal fusion as a pathway forward, yet existing fusion approaches face critical challenges that limit their effectiveness.

\subsection{Challenges in Multi-Modal Snow Cover Estimation}

Current fusion strategies predominantly employ either early fusion (concatenating raw sensor data) or late fusion (combining independent predictions), both of which fail to capture the complex, non-linear relationships between SAR backscatter patterns and optical spectral signatures \cite{yu2025stsnet}. Early fusion treats different modalities as simply additional channels, ignoring their fundamentally different physical measurement principles, while late fusion lacks mechanisms for cross-modal information exchange during feature learning. Beyond methodological limitations, operational satellite missions exhibit inherent multi-resolution characteristics that create fundamental training-inference gaps. While Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 provide data at 10--20m resolution, many reference snow cover products, including widely-used datasets like MODIS and the Copernicus Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) product, are available only at coarser resolutions ($\geq$60m) \cite{hoppinen2024evaluating}. Models trained on coarse-resolution targets may fail to leverage fine-scale information available in high-resolution input data, while direct downscaling approaches often introduce artifacts and fail to preserve physical consistency. Real-world satellite observations also exhibit highly variable quality characteristics due to atmospheric conditions, sensor calibration drift, geometric distortions, and processing artifacts \cite{poussin2025snow}. Traditional machine learning approaches treat all training samples equally, effectively assuming homogeneous data quality, an assumption systematically violated in operational satellite data streams. This quality heterogeneity can lead to degraded model performance and poor generalization when models encounter data quality conditions not well-represented in training sets. Furthermore, most existing approaches lack explicit uncertainty quantification, providing point predictions without reliability indicators essential for operational decision-making in critical applications such as flood forecasting and water resource planning.

Recent advances in deep learning, particularly attention mechanisms and vision transformer architectures, provide new opportunities for addressing these challenges \cite{hidalgo2024synergistic, ding2023multi}. The key insight driving our work is that effective multi-modal fusion requires explicit modeling of cross-modal relationships, understanding not just what each sensor observes independently, but how observations from different sensors relate to and inform each other. However, adapting these techniques to earth observation requires careful consideration of unique characteristics: high-dimensional data, spatial correlation structures, and physical constraints that differ fundamentally from natural language or computer vision domains where transformers were originally developed.

\subsection{Contributions}

\deleted{This work introduces a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR (10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 bands unified at 10m) to generate high-resolution fractional snow cover maps with calibrated uncertainty estimates. Our approach addresses the identified challenges through three principal methodological innovations. First, we develop a novel multi-resolution processing framework that operates at 10m input resolution, aggregates features to 60m for supervised training against GFSC reference data, and produces enhanced 10m predictions through guided disaggregation, enabling training on widely-available coarse-resolution labels while exploiting fine-scale input information. Second, we design a cross-attention fusion mechanism that explicitly models bidirectional dependencies between SAR backscatter and optical spectral responses, capturing complex inter-sensor relationships missed by traditional concatenation or ensemble approaches. Third, we incorporate quality-aware training with multiplicative loss weighting based on multi-source metadata (GFSC QC layers, Sentinel-2 AOT/WVP/SCL), enabling robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data while providing well-calibrated uncertainty estimates. The hybrid architecture combines dual-path CNN backbones for modality-specific feature extraction, cross-attention fusion for intelligent multi-modal integration, and spatial transformers (6 layers, 8 heads, 512-dim) for global context modeling. Quality-aware training employs adaptive loss weighting ($\text{weight} = (\text{confidence}^\alpha) \times (\text{quality\_mask}^\beta)$) with curriculum learning that progressively relaxes quality thresholds during training. The uncertainty quantification framework provides spatially coherent confidence estimates that enable targeted quality control and risk-informed decision-making in downstream applications.}

\deleted{We provide comprehensive evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland with rigorous 70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test split and 30-day temporal separation, evaluating on 18,117 patches from 4 unseen geographic tiles. Our model achieves state-of-the-art performance: 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines trained on identical data. Compared to CNN-Only baseline (89.13\% accuracy, 22.46\% MAE), our hybrid architecture achieves 38.7\% MAE reduction (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Early Fusion (simple concatenation) achieves only 83.98\% accuracy with 25.20\% MAE, while Transformer-Only reaches 85.35\% accuracy with 27.05\% MAE, validating the necessity of hybrid CNN-Transformer design. Statistical validation confirms strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$; $R^2=0.588$ explaining 58.8\% variance) and physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$), substantially exceeding baselines: CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). The 24.9 percentage point improvement in explained variance over CNN-Only ($R^2$: 0.239 $\rightarrow$ 0.588) represents 2.5$\times$ better performance. Systematic ablation studies validate each component: removing cross-attention causes 5.16 point accuracy drop (largest individual contribution), removing transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points, demonstrating synergistic benefits of the hybrid design. Critically, uncertainty quantification achieves excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025, well below the 0.03 threshold, with mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching observed error rate and spatially coherent patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$) with elevated values at snow-land boundaries. Test-time augmentation enhances accuracy by 2.3\% with 22.4\% reduction in uncertainty variance. While comprehensive comparative evaluation against operational products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F), multi-regional assessment across diverse climatic conditions, and multi-season temporal validation remain essential future work for operational deployment, the current evaluation demonstrates architectural superiority over strong baselines and excellent calibration properties. The methodological contributions include multi-resolution training, cross-modal attention fusion, and quality-aware learning, which have broad implications for earth observation applications beyond snow monitoring, addressing fundamental challenges in learning from heterogeneous satellite data with resolution mismatches and variable quality characteristics.}

\added{This work introduces XFuse, a hybrid CNN--Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical imagery to generate fractional snow cover maps with calibrated uncertainty estimates.} \\
\added{The proposed approach addresses the identified challenges through three principal contributions:} \\
\added{1. \textbf{Bidirectional cross-attention fusion} that models asymmetric SAR--optical interdependencies through learned query--key--value projections, enabling each modality to selectively attend to complementary information from the other. Unlike concatenation or ensemble strategies that treat modalities symmetrically or independently, cross-attention captures how SAR structural information informs optical interpretation under cloud contamination, and how optical spectral signatures disambiguate SAR backscatter variations.} \\
\added{2. \textbf{Multi-resolution training strategy} ($10\,\mathrm{m} \rightarrow 60\,\mathrm{m} \rightarrow 10\,\mathrm{m}$) that fuses Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 features at native resolution, adaptively aggregates them to 60\,m for supervised training against GFSC reference labels, and subsequently disaggregates predictions through guided upsampling for spatial enhancement. This enables training with widely available coarse-resolution reference data while preserving fine-scale spatial information from high-resolution inputs.} \\
\added{3. \textbf{Quality-aware curriculum learning} that integrates heterogeneous metadata from multiple independent sources (Sentinel-2 cloud masks and atmospheric parameters, together with GFSC quality flags) via multiplicative loss weighting with progressive threshold scheduling. This strategy enables robust learning from variable-quality satellite observations while producing well-calibrated uncertainty estimates suitable for operational decision support.} \\
\added{Evaluation on Sentinel-1/2 data from northern Finland using geographically separated test tiles demonstrates state-of-the-art performance, achieving a 38.7\% reduction in MAE compared to the strongest baseline. Systematic ablation studies show that cross-attention fusion provides the largest individual performance gain, validating the proposed architectural design. Full experimental results, statistical validation, and uncertainty analysis are presented in Section~\ref{sec:result}.}


\subsection{Paper Organization}

The remainder of this paper is organised as follows. Section \ref{sec:related_work} reviews related work in multi-modal remote sensing, deep learning architectures for earth observation, and quality-aware machine learning. Section \ref{sec:proposed_method} presents our methodology, including detailed architectural descriptions and training procedures. Section \ref{sec:result} describes experimental setup, datasets, and evaluation protocols and presents comprehensive results including quantitative performance analysis and uncertainty quantification. Section \ref{sec:discussion}  discusses implications, limitations, and future research directions. Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes with a summary of contributions and broader impacts.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Interpretation of Key Results}

The hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal satellite fusion achieved state-of-the-art performance across all evaluation metrics. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy on 18,117 test patches from 4 geographically separated tiles, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). These accuracy levels substantially exceed established operational requirements for snow water equivalent modeling, where estimation errors below 15-20\% are generally acceptable for hydrological forecasting applications \cite{hall2002modis, gascoin2019theia}, achieving approximately 5\% residual error rate (100\% - 94.66\%) suitable for critical operational deployments. The correlation coefficient of $r=0.784$ ($p<0.001$) between predictions and GFSC reference data demonstrates strong model calibration and predictive accuracy for continuous fractional snow cover estimation. This correlation strength ($R^2=0.588$) is particularly significant given that the model operates at 10m resolution while training on 60m reference data, indicating successful multi-resolution knowledge transfer. The combination of high correlation, substantial explained variance (58.8\%), and low error metrics (MAE: 13.77\%, RMSE: 0.180) confirms precise quantitative predictions suitable for operational applications requiring fine-grained snow cover fraction estimates.

Critically, the uncertainty quantification framework demonstrates excellent calibration properties with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025, well below the 0.03 threshold for well-calibrated models. Mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matches the observed $\sim$10\% error rate (100\% - 89.52\% accuracy during validation), confirming that the model's confidence estimates accurately reflect prediction reliability. Spatially coherent uncertainty patterns exhibit elevated uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries (Moran's I = 0.548, p = 0.002), demonstrating physically realistic uncertainty estimation with moderate spatial autocorrelation that aligns with known sources of ambiguity in remote sensing-based snow detection. These well-calibrated uncertainty estimates provide decision-makers with trustworthy quantitative confidence bounds for applications ranging from avalanche risk assessment to water resource planning.

\subsection{Baseline Comparison Insights}

The systematic comparison with strong baseline architectures validates each key design choice in our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture. Early Fusion (simple concatenation) achieves only 83.98\% accuracy, demonstrating that treating heterogeneous modalities as additional channels fails to capture the complex, non-linear relationships between SAR backscatter and optical spectral signatures. CNN-Only improves to 89.13\% accuracy by employing modality-specific feature extraction, but still falls 5.5 percentage points short of our hybrid model, indicating that pure CNN architectures lack the global context modeling necessary for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns influenced by topography and regional climate. Transformer-Only achieves only 85.35\% accuracy, underperforming even Early Fusion, which validates that local feature extraction through CNNs is essential for capturing fine-scale spatial patterns and precise boundary localization in fractional snow cover estimation.

The substantial performance gap between our hybrid architecture (94.66\% accuracy) and the best individual component (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) demonstrates synergistic benefits rather than simple additive improvements. The 5.5 percentage point accuracy gain represents a 48.7\% reduction in error rate (10.87\% $\rightarrow$ 5.34\%), confirming that CNNs and Transformers provide genuinely complementary capabilities: CNNs excel at extracting hierarchical spatial features and local texture patterns, while Transformers capture long-range dependencies and global context essential for understanding spatially distributed snow cover influenced by elevation gradients, aspect variations, and regional meteorological patterns. The ablation studies further validate this conclusion, showing that removing either cross-attention (5.16 point drop) or the transformer (3.86 point drop) causes substantial performance degradation, while removing both (reverting to Early Fusion baseline) results in catastrophic 10.68 point accuracy loss.

Statistical quality metrics further demonstrate the hybrid architecture's superiority. Our model achieves $R^2=0.588$ (58.8\% explained variance), substantially exceeding CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), and Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). The 24.9 percentage point improvement in explained variance over CNN-Only ($R^2$: 0.239 $\rightarrow$ 0.588) represents 2.5$\times$ better performance, confirming that the hybrid design captures substantially more of the underlying snow cover variance through its combination of local feature extraction and global context modeling.

\subsection{Multi-Resolution Processing Effectiveness}

  \begin{figure}[!h]
      \centering
      \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/multi_resolution_schematic.png}
      \caption{Multi-resolution processing framework schematic. The novel training strategy operates at three resolution stages: (1) high-resolution input fusion at 10m combining Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data through hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture, (2) adaptive aggregation to 60m for supervised training against GFSC  reference data with quality-aware loss weighting, and (3) guided disaggregation back to 10m for enhanced spatial detail prediction during   inference. This approach enables training on widely-available coarse-resolution labels while producing high-resolution outputs that preserve fine-scale spatial patterns absent in training targets.}
      \label{fig:multi_resolution_schematic}
  \end{figure}

The novel $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy represents a significant methodological advancement that addresses fundamental limitations in earth observation where high-resolution sensors lack corresponding high-resolution reference data. As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:multi_resolution_schematic}, the multi-resolution framework enabled the model to leverage fine-scale spatial information from Sentinel-1 (10m) and Sentinel-2 (10m unified resolution) while training against coarser GFSC reference data (60m), subsequently producing enhanced 10m predictions that preserve spatial detail absent in the training targets. The effectiveness of this approach is evidenced by the model's ability to generate spatially coherent high-resolution outputs that maintain consistency with coarse-resolution supervision while achieving excellent performance metrics (accuracy $>$89\%, correlation r=0.881). The guided disaggregation mechanism, employing spatial correlation modeling and bilateral upsampling, successfully recovered fine-scale snow cover patterns that would be undetectable through simple interpolation of 60m reference data. The model's consistent performance across diverse snow coverage scenarios (0.2\%-75.5\%) confirms its capacity to reconstruct sub-pixel detail guided by high-resolution input features.

This multi-resolution strategy offers several advantages over traditional single-resolution approaches. First, it enables training on widely available coarse-resolution reference GFSC data while still producing high-resolution outputs that exploit the full spatial capacity of modern satellite sensors. Second, the learned aggregation functions preserve critical spatial information during downsampling, avoiding information loss inherent in fixed aggregation methods like simple averaging. Third, the approach is generalizable to other earth observation applications facing similar resolution mismatches between high-resolution input sensors and coarse-resolution reference data. The trade-offs of this approach merit consideration. Training on coarse-resolution targets may limit the model's ability to capture sub-60m snow cover features that lack representation in the training signal. However, the incorporation of high-resolution auxiliary features (NDSI, backscatter textures) and the guided disaggregation framework partially mitigate this limitation by providing fine-scale structural information to guide the upsampling process. Future work could explore hybrid training strategies that combine coarse-resolution reference data with sparse high-resolution annotations to further enhance sub-pixel prediction capabilities.


\subsection{Cross-Modal Fusion Performance}

The architecture's multi-modal fusion design combines Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data to achieve excellent performance (accuracy: 89.52\%, F1-score: 90.85\%), leveraging the complementary information captured by SAR and optical modalities. SAR provides all-weather capability with sensitivity to surface roughness and moisture, while optical sensors offer rich spectral information for snow discrimination. The achieved performance demonstrates that intelligent fusion of these modalities enables accurate snow cover estimation across diverse conditions. The cross-attention fusion mechanism enables explicit modeling of bidirectional dependencies between SAR backscatter patterns and optical spectral signatures. The attention mechanism allows each modality to dynamically weight the relevance of information from the complementary modality, adapting to varying data quality and snow conditions. This design choice supports the model's robust performance across the full range of snow coverage scenarios tested (0.2\%-75.5\%), from near-snow-free conditions where optical information dominates to cloud-contaminated scenarios where SAR provides critical all-weather observations. The model's consistent performance across varying atmospheric conditions validates the fusion architecture's effectiveness at leveraging complementary sensor information. The quality-aware training framework further enhances fusion robustness by appropriately weighting samples based on data quality metadata, enabling the model to learn optimal sensor utilization patterns under diverse observation conditions.


\subsection{Quality-Aware Training Impact}

The quality-aware training framework contributes to the model's excellent overall performance (accuracy: 89.52\%, F1-score: 90.85\%) by enabling robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data with varying quality characteristics. Explicit incorporation of data quality information through multiplicative loss weighting enables the model to learn representations that generalize effectively across challenging atmospheric conditions and varying snow coverage scenarios (0.2\%-75.5\%). The integration of auxiliary metadata from multiple sources (Sentinel-2 AOT, WVP, SCL; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) provided complementary quality indicators that collectively enhanced training efficiency. The adaptive weighting scheme $\text{weight} = (\text{confidence}^\alpha) \times (\text{quality\_mask}^\beta)$ effectively balanced sample diversity with quality emphasis, avoiding the pitfall of training exclusively on high-quality samples while still prioritizing reliable data.

The curriculum learning component, progressively relaxing confidence thresholds from $\tau_{init}=0.9$ to $\tau_{final}=0.3$, proved particularly valuable for handling the heterogeneous quality characteristics of satellite data. This progressive strategy enabled stable learning dynamics during early training while gradually exposing the model to more challenging scenarios, ultimately improving robustness to edge cases and degraded data conditions. The operational implications of quality-aware training are significant. Models trained with quality awareness demonstrate superior performance under real-world conditions where data quality varies spatially and temporally. The uncertainty estimates enable operational systems to automatically flag low-confidence predictions, reducing the risk of erroneous decisions in critical applications. Furthermore, the quality-aware framework facilitates efficient utilization of large but noisy satellite archives by extracting maximum value from available data rather than requiring extensive manual curation.

Limitations of the current quality-aware approach include reliance on pre-defined quality indicators that may not capture all sources of data degradation. Future work could explore learned quality assessment networks that automatically identify degraded data based on input characteristics, potentially improving robustness to unanticipated quality issues.


\subsection{Uncertainty Quantification and Reliability}

The uncertainty quantification framework provides operationally valuable insights into prediction reliability through spatially coherent uncertainty estimates. The framework successfully identifies regions where predictions are less reliable, with elevated uncertainty at snow-land boundaries and areas with complex topography, demonstrating physically realistic uncertainty estimation that aligns with known sources of ambiguity in snow detection. The operational value of uncertainty quantification extends beyond simple confidence flagging. Quantified uncertainties enable probabilistic decision-making frameworks where downstream applications can incorporate prediction uncertainty into risk assessments. For example, flood forecasting models could weight snow cover predictions by their uncertainty estimates, propagating uncertainty through hydrological models to generate probabilistic runoff predictions. Similarly, avalanche risk assessment systems could flag high-uncertainty regions for additional monitoring or ground validation.

Uncertainty analysis revealed that prediction confidence appropriately varies with data quality, surface complexity, and snow coverage characteristics. Elevated uncertainty in regions with limited training data representation (complex topography, rare surface types) or high atmospheric interference (cloud contamination, sensor noise) enables targeted quality control and adaptive processing strategies. This pattern enables targeted improvement strategies: increasing training data diversity for underrepresented scenarios and improving preprocessing algorithms to reduce uncertainty from atmospheric and sensor artifacts. The uncertainty framework's ability to appropriately increase estimates for out-of-distribution scenarios enhances operational reliability by flagging predictions that may require additional validation. Cross-domain validation confirmed effective separation of high-confidence from low-confidence predictions, reducing the risk of overconfident errors in atypical conditions.

