AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) with curriculum learning, enabling robust training from heterogeneous satellite data while providing calibrated uncertainty estimates. Comprehensive evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland (70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test with 30-day temporal separation, 18,117 test patches from 4 unseen tiles) demonstrates state-of-the-art performance. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Statistical validation confirms strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$; $R^2=0.588$ explaining 58.8\% variance) and physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$), substantially exceeding baselines (CNN-Only: $R^2=0.239$; Early Fusion: $R^2=0.026$). Systematic ablation studies validate each component's contribution: removing cross-attention causes 5.16 point accuracy drop, removing transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points. Critically, uncertainty quantification demonstrates excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025 $<$ 0.03 threshold, mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching the observed error rate, and spatially coherent patterns with elevated values at snow-land boundaries (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$). Test-time augmentation improves accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%. The methodological contributions in multi-resolution training, cross-modal attention fusion, quality-aware learning, have broad implications for earth observation applications requiring fusion of heterogeneous multi-resolution satellite data with quantified uncertainty.}
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/0_abstract.tex:4:\added{Accurate fractional snow cover (FSC) mapping requires combining the complementary strengths of optical imagery (spectral discrimination) and Synthetic Aperture Radar (all-weather capability), yet existing fusion approaches employ simplistic concatenation strategies that fail to model complex inter-modal relationships, while ignoring the resolution mismatch between high-resolution inputs and coarse reference labels. We introduce XFuse, a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture that addresses these challenges through three key contributions: (1) \textbf{bidirectional cross-attention fusion} that explicitly models SAR-optical interdependencies via query-key-value transformations, enabling each modality to selectively attend to complementary information from the other; (2) a \textbf{multi-resolution training strategy} ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) that fuses Sentinel-1/2 features at native resolution, aggregates to 60m for supervision against Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) labels, and disaggregates through guided upsampling for enhanced spatial detail; and (3) \textbf{quality-aware curriculum learning} that incorporates multi-source metadata (cloud masks, atmospheric parameters, QC flags) through adaptive loss weighting, enabling robust training from heterogeneous satellite data while providing calibrated uncertainty estimates. Evaluation on 29 Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland (18,117 test patches from 4 geographically unseen tiles) demonstrates state-of-the-art performance: 13.77\% MAE and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing 38.7\% MAE reduction over the best baseline. Ablation studies confirm cross-attention as the largest individual contributor (5.16 percentage point accuracy gain), while uncertainty quantification achieves excellent calibration (ECE=0.025). The methodology generalizes to earth observation applications requiring fusion of heterogeneous multi-resolution satellite data.}
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/1_intro.tex:4:Snow cover represents one of the most dynamic and influential components of the Earth's cryosphere, affecting an estimated 1.9 billion people who depend on snow-fed rivers for their water supply \cite{mankin2015potential}. Acting as both a massive freshwater reservoir and a critical climate regulator through its high albedo, snow cover exerts profound influence on regional and global climate systems, hydrological cycles, and socioeconomic systems \cite{siirila2021low, avanzi2024winter}. The seasonal snow cover extent has decreased significantly over recent decades, with Northern Hemisphere spring snow cover declining at rates of 1.6\% per decade for March-April and up to 11.7\% per decade for June since 1967 \cite{mudryk2020historical}, making accurate, timely snow monitoring essential for understanding and adapting to climate change impacts. Operational requirements for snow monitoring are demanding: high spatial resolution to capture fine-scale heterogeneity in mountainous terrain, frequent temporal coverage to track rapid snowmelt events, and robust all-weather capability to ensure continuity during storm events when snow accumulation is most dynamic.
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/1_intro.tex:18:\deleted{We provide comprehensive evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland with rigorous 70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test split and 30-day temporal separation, evaluating on 18,117 patches from 4 unseen geographic tiles. Our model achieves state-of-the-art performance: 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines trained on identical data. Compared to CNN-Only baseline (89.13\% accuracy, 22.46\% MAE), our hybrid architecture achieves 38.7\% MAE reduction (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Early Fusion (simple concatenation) achieves only 83.98\% accuracy with 25.20\% MAE, while Transformer-Only reaches 85.35\% accuracy with 27.05\% MAE, validating the necessity of hybrid CNN-Transformer design. Statistical validation confirms strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$; $R^2=0.588$ explaining 58.8\% variance) and physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$), substantially exceeding baselines: CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). The 24.9 percentage point improvement in explained variance over CNN-Only ($R^2$: 0.239 $\rightarrow$ 0.588) represents 2.5$\times$ better performance. Systematic ablation studies validate each component: removing cross-attention causes 5.16 point accuracy drop (largest individual contribution), removing transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points, demonstrating synergistic benefits of the hybrid design. Critically, uncertainty quantification achieves excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025, well below the 0.03 threshold, with mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching observed error rate and spatially coherent patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$) with elevated values at snow-land boundaries. Test-time augmentation enhances accuracy by 2.3\% with 22.4\% reduction in uncertainty variance. While comprehensive comparative evaluation against operational products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F), multi-regional assessment across diverse climatic conditions, and multi-season temporal validation remain essential future work for operational deployment, the current evaluation demonstrates architectural superiority over strong baselines and excellent calibration properties. The methodological contributions include multi-resolution training, cross-modal attention fusion, and quality-aware learning, which have broad implications for earth observation applications beyond snow monitoring, addressing fundamental challenges in learning from heterogeneous satellite data with resolution mismatches and variable quality characteristics.}
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/1_intro.tex:25:\added{Evaluation on Sentinel-1/2 data from northern Finland using geographically separated test tiles demonstrates state-of-the-art performance, achieving a 38.7\% reduction in MAE compared to the strongest baseline. Systematic ablation studies show that cross-attention fusion provides the largest individual performance gain, validating the proposed architectural design. Full experimental results, statistical validation, and uncertainty analysis are presented in Section~\ref{sec:result}.}
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/2_Related_Work.tex:5:Estimating snow cover fraction (SCF) from satellite imagery poses a critical challenge at the intersection of remote sensing and machine learning, particularly in complex mountainous terrain where cloud cover, forest canopy, and topographic shadows confound traditional approaches. The evolution from single-sensor optical methods to multimodal fusion architectures reflects both technological advancements and a deeper understanding of the complementary information embedded in different spectral and polarimetric signatures. Early optical-based approaches leveraging MODIS and VIIRS sensors established foundational methodologies for snow detection through the Normalized Difference Snow Index (NDSI), exploiting snow's distinctive spectral signature with high visible reflectance and low shortwave infrared response \citep{hall2002modis, cover2017algorithm}. However, persistent cloud cover in mountainous regions creates critical data gaps, with studies reporting up to 60\% temporal coverage loss during winter months \citep{parajka2008spatio}, necessitating complementary all-weather observation capabilities.
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/4_Result.tex:8:\textbf{Dataset and Data Splits.} We train and evaluate our hybrid CNN-Transformer fusion architecture on multi-modal remote sensing data combining Sentinel-1 GRDH SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m resolution) and Sentinel-2B/C optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m from native 10m/20m/60m resolutions) \cite{CopernicusDashboard}. Ground truth labels use Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) at 60m resolution \cite{CLMS_SnowCover_2016present}. The dataset comprises 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tile acquisitions from northern Finland spanning diverse conditions: snow coverage 0--100\%, varying cloud coverage, and heterogeneous land cover. We employ a geographic split to ensure spatial independence: 70\% training (12 tiles, $\sim$54,000 patches), 15\% validation (3 tiles, $\sim$9,000 patches), and 15\% test (4 tiles, 18,117 patches). The 30-day temporal separation between splits prevents data leakage. Data preprocessing includes SAR radiometric calibration to sigma-naught backscatter, Enhanced Lee Sigma speckle filtering (7$\times$7 window), Range-Doppler terrain correction, and atmospheric correction for optical bands using a hybrid Dark Object Subtraction and MODTRAN-based approach incorporating Rayleigh scattering, aerosol transmission (AOT at 550nm), and water vapor absorption corrections.
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/4_Result.tex:17:\added{\textbf{Data Acquisition Details.} Table~\ref{tab:acquisitions} summarises the satellite datasets and geographic coverage to support reproducibility. All Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 scenes are freely accessible from the ESA Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem}\footnotemark[1]; \added{GFSC composites are available from the Copernicus Land Monitoring Service HRWSI portal}\footnotemark[2]. \added{The raw data archive comprises 178 co-registered Sentinel-1/Sentinel-2 acquisition pairs (407\,GB total) collected from December 2024 to July 2025, spanning 45 MGRS tiles across Finland (60--70.5\textdegree{}N, 20--32\textdegree{}E, UTM zones 33--36) with a maximum Sentinel-2 cloud coverage threshold of 20\% applied at retrieval time. After quality-controlled preprocessing---including SAR radiometric calibration, terrain correction, optical atmospheric correction, and geometric co-registration---28 W-band (northern Finland, 64--72\textdegree{}N) tiles with complete processing pipelines were retained for training data construction. Assembling the final dataset required temporally matching three independently scheduled data streams: Sentinel-1 (6-day revisit cycle), Sentinel-2 (5-day revisit cycle), and GFSC 7-day composites. Differences in revisit periods, orbital geometries, viewing angles, and composite dating windows substantially constrain the number of valid co-temporal combinations; after applying a strict 30-day temporal window for triplet alignment, 29 matched (S1, S2, GFSC) triplets from 19 unique W-band MGRS tiles were obtained. These triplets span July--October 2025, covering the transition from the snow-free summer through autumn snow onset in northern Lapland, and together encompass the full range of fractional snow cover conditions (0--100\%) present in the dataset.}
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/4_Result.tex:26:\caption{\added{Dataset summary by sensor: raw archive size, geographic coverage, and key acquisition parameters. The raw archive of 178 co-registered S1/S2 pairs (407\,GB) was reduced to 29 matched triplets after quality-controlled preprocessing and strict temporal co-registration. GFSC: Copernicus CLMS HRWSI Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover, version V102. W-band tiles cover northern Finland (64--72\textdegree{}N).}}
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/4_Result.tex:322:\textbf{Dataset Scope.} Our test set covers \deleted{4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025}\added{4 geographically distinct W-band MGRS tiles from northern Finland (July--October 2025)}, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. Future work should evaluate on multi-season, multi-region datasets to assess year-round operational readiness and transferability to different snow conditions (maritime vs. continental snow, high-altitude vs. lowland environments).
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/5_Discussion.tex:56:    \item \textbf{Dataset Scope:} Our test set covers \deleted{4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025}\added{4 geographically distinct W-band MGRS tiles from northern Finland (July--October 2025)}, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region through rigorous 70/15/15 train/val/test split with 30-day temporal separation. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. The test set's 18,117 patches from unseen tiles provide strong evidence for spatial transferability, but year-round operational readiness requires multi-season evaluation. Future work should assess performance in mid-latitude regions with sporadic snow cover, forested environments with canopy interference, maritime vs. continental snow regimes, and high-altitude vs. lowland environments.
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/5_Discussion.tex:58:    \item \added{\textbf{Geographic Transferability:} Our study area-northern Finland's boreal zone-features relatively flat to gently undulating terrain (typical slopes $<$5\textdegree), homogeneous boreal forest land cover, and predominantly dry continental snow. These conditions represent a favorable scenario for SAR-based fusion: gentle topography minimizes SAR geometric distortions (layover, foreshortening, radar shadow), and dry snow exhibits relatively stable C-band backscatter characteristics \cite{ulaby1986microwave, nagler2000improved}. Consequently, our reported performance metrics should be interpreted as achievable under near-optimal SAR acquisition geometry, and transferability to other geographic and climatic settings cannot be assumed without dedicated validation. \\ 
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/5_Discussion.tex:59:    In complex alpine terrain (e.g., European Alps, Himalayas, Andes), three interacting factors would present substantial challenges. \textit{First}, SAR geometric distortions scale nonlinearly with terrain slope relative to the radar look angle. At Sentinel-1's nominal incidence angles (29\textdegree--46\textdegree), foreslopes exceeding approximately 20\textdegree\ produce severe foreshortening where topographic relief is compressed in the range direction, while slopes approaching the complement of the incidence angle cause layover, where returns from multiple elevations collapse into the same range cell, rendering affected pixels physically uninterpretable \cite{tsai2019remote}. Radar shadow on steep back-slopes creates complete signal voids. Critically, these distortions concentrate in high-altitude accumulation zones where accurate snow monitoring is most needed for downstream water resource forecasting \cite{immerzeel2010large}. \textit{Second}, snow metamorphism varies substantially across climatic regimes, producing fundamentally different SAR-optical signatures. Maritime snowpacks (e.g., Scandinavian coast, Cascades) are warm, dense, and undergo frequent melt--freeze cycles that cause strong C-band absorption through liquid water content \cite{nagler2016advancements}; continental interior snowpacks (e.g., Rocky Mountains, Siberia) remain cold and dry with depth hoar and faceted grain growth exhibiting volume scattering; and transitional regimes (e.g., Himalayan basins) display complex diurnal melt--refreeze stratification varying with elevation \cite{snapir2019spatiotemporal}. Feature representations learned from northern Finland's predominantly dry boreal snow may therefore not generalize to regimes where SAR-snow interactions are governed by different physical mechanisms. \textit{Third}, the strong coupling between elevation, aspect, solar illumination geometry, and snow distribution in mountainous terrain creates spatial heterogeneity at scales far finer than observed in our study area, where snow distribution is governed primarily by vegetation canopy density and latitude rather than topographic position. \\  Several architectural properties of XFuse are relevant to these challenges, though their effectiveness in alpine settings remains unvalidated. The bidirectional cross-attention mechanism enables each modality to selectively attend to the other through learned query-key-value projections-in SAR-degraded regions, the attention weights could theoretically shift toward optical spectral features, analogous to the mechanism's designed behavior under cloud contamination but operating in the reverse direction. The quality-aware training framework, which learns continuous sample weighting from heterogeneous metadata, provides a natural extension point: terrain-derived quality indicators (local incidence angle, layover/shadow masks from DEM analysis) could be incorporated as additional metadata channels, enabling systematic down-weighting of geometrically distorted SAR observations. However, these remain architectural affordances rather than validated capabilities. \\  Robust geographic generalization would require: (1) radiometric terrain correction of Sentinel-1 data using high-resolution DEMs to mitigate slope-dependent radiometric biases; (2) incorporation of topographic variables (slope, aspect, curvature, sky view factor) as auxiliary model inputs through a dedicated terrain encoder path; (3) training on geographically diverse datasets spanning boreal, alpine, and maritime snow regimes to capture regime-dependent SAR-optical relationships; and (4) domain adaptation or transfer learning strategies to efficiently extend learned representations to undersampled snow climate zones. We consider multi-region validation across contrasting terrain types and snow climate regimes as the primary direction for extending this work toward globally applicable operational deployment.}
