coherent patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$) with elevated values at snow-land boundaries. Test-time augmentation enhances accuracy by 2.3\% with 22.4\% reduction in uncertainty variance. While comprehensive comparative evaluation against operational products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F), multi-regional assessment across diverse climatic conditions, and multi-season temporal validation remain essential future work for operational deployment, the current evaluation demonstrates architectural superiority over strong baselines and excellent calibration properties. The methodological contributions include multi-resolution training, cross-modal attention fusion, and quality-aware learning, which have broad implications for earth observation applications beyond snow monitoring, addressing fundamental challenges in learning from heterogeneous satellite data with resolution mismatches and variable quality characteristics.}
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/4_Result.tex:8:\textbf{Dataset and Data Splits.} We train and evaluate our hybrid CNN-Transformer fusion architecture on multi-modal remote sensing data combining Sentinel-1 GRDH SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m resolution) and Sentinel-2B/C optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m from native 10m/20m/60m resolutions) \cite{CopernicusDashboard}. Ground truth labels use Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) at 60m resolution \cite{CLMS_SnowCover_2016present}. The dataset comprises 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tile acquisitions from northern Finland spanning diverse conditions: snow coverage 0--100\%, varying cloud coverage, and heterogeneous land cover. We employ a geographic split to ensure spatial independence: 70\% training (12 tiles, $\sim$54,000 patches), 15\% validation (3 tiles, $\sim$9,000 patches), and 15\% test (4 tiles, 18,117 patches). The 30-day temporal separation between splits prevents data leakage. Data preprocessing includes SAR radiometric calibration to sigma-naught backscatter, Enhanced Lee Sigma speckle filtering (7$\times$7 window), Range-Doppler terrain correction, and atmospheric correction for optical bands using a hybrid Dark Object Subtraction and MODTRAN-based approach incorporating Rayleigh scattering, aerosol transmission (AOT at 550nm), and water vapor absorption corrections.
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/4_Result.tex:17:\added{\textbf{Data Acquisition Details.} Table~\ref{tab:acquisitions} summarises the satellite datasets and geographic coverage to support reproducibility. All Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 scenes are freely accessible from the ESA Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem}\footnotemark[1]; \added{GFSC composites are available from the Copernicus Land Monitoring Service HRWSI portal}\footnotemark[2]. \added{The raw data archive comprises 178 co-registered Sentinel-1/Sentinel-2 acquisition pairs (407\,GB total) collected from December 2024 to July 2025, spanning 45 MGRS tiles across Finland (60--70.5\textdegree{}N, 20--32\textdegree{}E, UTM zones 33--36) with a maximum Sentinel-2 cloud coverage threshold of 20\% applied at retrieval time. After quality-controlled preprocessing---including SAR radiometric calibration, terrain correction, optical atmospheric correction, and geometric co-registration---28 W-band (northern Finland, 64--72\textdegree{}N) tiles with complete processing pipelines were retained for training data construction. Assembling the final dataset required temporally matching three independently scheduled data streams: Sentinel-1 (6-day revisit cycle), Sentinel-2 (5-day revisit cycle), and GFSC 7-day composites. Differences in revisit periods, orbital geometries, viewing angles, and composite dating windows substantially constrain the number of valid co-temporal combinations; after applying a strict 30-day temporal window for triplet alignment, 29 matched (S1, S2, GFSC) triplets from 19 unique W-band MGRS tiles were obtained. These triplets span July--October 2025, covering the transition from the snow-free summer through autumn snow onset in northern Lapland, and together encompass the full range of fractional snow cover conditions (0--100\%) present in the dataset.}
