/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/4_Result.tex:316:\textbf{Inference Speed.} The model processes 16$\times$16 patches (960m$\times$960m ground coverage at 60m resolution) with $\sim$50ms inference time per patch on A100 GPU. For the entire test set (18,117 patches), inference requires $\sim$15 minutes. Batch processing with batch size 32 achieves throughput of $\sim$2,000 patches/minute, enabling rapid large-scale deployment. Peak GPU memory consumption is 4.2GB during inference, allowing deployment on consumer-grade hardware.
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/4_Result.tex:322:\textbf{Dataset Scope.} Our test set covers \deleted{4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025}\added{4 geographically distinct W-band MGRS tiles from northern Finland (July--October 2025)}, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. Future work should evaluate on multi-season, multi-region datasets to assess year-round operational readiness and transferability to different snow conditions (maritime vs. continental snow, high-altitude vs. lowland environments).
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/5_Discussion.tex:6:The hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal satellite fusion achieved state-of-the-art performance across all evaluation metrics. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy on 18,117 test patches from 4 geographically separated tiles, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). These accuracy levels substantially exceed established operational requirements for snow water equivalent modeling, where estimation errors below 15-20\% are generally acceptable for hydrological forecasting applications \cite{hall2002modis, gascoin2019theia}, achieving approximately 5\% residual error rate (100\% - 94.66\%) suitable for critical operational deployments. The correlation coefficient of $r=0.784$ ($p<0.001$) between predictions and GFSC reference data demonstrates strong model calibration and predictive accuracy for continuous fractional snow cover estimation. This correlation strength ($R^2=0.588$) is particularly significant given that the model operates at 10m resolution while training on 60m reference data, indicating successful multi-resolution knowledge transfer. The combination of high correlation, substantial explained variance (58.8\%), and low error metrics (MAE: 13.77\%, RMSE: 0.180) confirms precise quantitative predictions suitable for operational applications requiring fine-grained snow cover fraction estimates.
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/5_Discussion.tex:56:    \item \textbf{Dataset Scope:} Our test set covers \deleted{4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025}\added{4 geographically distinct W-band MGRS tiles from northern Finland (July--October 2025)}, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region through rigorous 70/15/15 train/val/test split with 30-day temporal separation. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. The test set's 18,117 patches from unseen tiles provide strong evidence for spatial transferability, but year-round operational readiness requires multi-season evaluation. Future work should assess performance in mid-latitude regions with sporadic snow cover, forested environments with canopy interference, maritime vs. continental snow regimes, and high-altitude vs. lowland environments.
/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/6_Conclusion.tex:6:Comprehensive experimental evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland with rigorous 70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test split and 30-day temporal separation demonstrated state-of-the-art performance on 18,117 test patches from 4 geographically unseen tiles. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy with 78.71\% F1-score, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Statistical validation confirmed strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$) with coefficient of determination $R^2=0.588$ (explaining 58.8\% variance), substantially exceeding baselines: CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). Spatial autocorrelation analysis (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$) validated physically realistic spatial patterns. Systematic ablation studies confirmed each component's contribution: removing cross-attention caused 5.16 point accuracy drop (largest individual contribution), removing transformer reduced accuracy by 3.86 points, demonstrating synergistic benefits of the hybrid design. Critically, uncertainty quantification achieved excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025 (well below the 0.03 threshold), mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching observed error rate, and spatially coherent patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$) with elevated values at snow-land boundaries. Test-time augmentation enhanced accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%. Computational efficiency analysis confirmed operational feasibility: 47.1M parameters, 4.2GB GPU memory during inference, $\sim$50ms per patch on A100 GPU, enabling throughput of $\sim$2,000 patches/minute with batch processing.

