/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/sec/0_abstract.tex:2:\deleted{Accurate fractional snow cover (FSC) estimation is critical for climate monitoring, hydrological modeling, and water resource management, yet current approaches face fundamental limitations. Optical sensors provide rich spectral information but are severely limited by cloud contamination, while Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) offers all-weather capability but lacks spectral richness for precise snow characterization. Existing multi-sensor fusion approaches either employ simplistic strategies like concatenation or ensemble that fail to capture complex inter-modal relationships, or ignore the inherent multi-resolution nature of operational satellite missions, limiting their ability to generate high-resolution predictions from coarse-resolution training labels. This paper introduces a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m from native 10m/20m/60m resolutions) to generate high-resolution fractional snow cover maps at 10m spatial resolution. The architecture addresses multi-resolution challenges through a novel $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy: S1/S2 features are fused at 10m through bidirectional cross-attention, aggregated to 60m for supervised training against Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) reference data, and disaggregated back to 10m through guided upsampling for enhanced spatial detail. Dual-path CNN backbones with modality-specific attention (CBAM for SAR, ECA for optical) extract hierarchical features, cross-attention fusion captures inter-sensor dependencies through explicit query-key-value transformations, and spatial transformers (6 layers, 8 heads, 512-dim) model global context. Quality-aware training incorporates multiplicative loss weighting based on multi-source metadata (Sentinel-2 SCL, 